\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:140:                    :span-kind (u8), :a (i32), :b (i32), :type (i32)}))\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-141-        failure {:kind (u8), :no-viable (u8), :index (i32), :buffered (u32)}\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:142:        tokens (counted (fn [] {:type (i32), :index (i32), :start (i32),\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-143-                                :stop (i32)}))]\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-144-    (when-not (= @pos length)\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-145-      (refuse \\\\\\\"trailing bytes\\\\\\\" {:consumed @pos, :length length}))\\\\n--\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-200-  {:token? (fn [i] (= :token (:kind (nth nodes i)))),\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-201-   :token (fn [i]\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-202-            (let [{:keys [type start stop text]} (nth nodes i)]\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:203:              {:type-name (type-name records type),\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-204-               :start start,\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-205-               :stop stop,\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-206-               :text text})),\\\\n--\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-258-   the first error failed on an end-of-input token, else `:early`\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-259-   (`export/failure-kind` decides).\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-260-\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:261:   The failing token follows the JVM rule: the offending token of a\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-262-   mismatch, and for a no-viable-alternative error the last token buffered\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-263-   when it was reported, since the C++ runtime's BufferedTokenStream fills\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-264-   lazily as the Java one does (observed against the JVM oracle in J0c round\\\"}]},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_2cb76ea87b1e4d1ab1074e34\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:21:                   \\\\\\\"Dart/Python3ParserBase.dart\\\\\\\" {:blake3 \\\\\\\"e4c4693a640e53b971d37088809c74539cc0f1dda7d9c6a3cdd9beb7ce40a87d\\\\\\\"},\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:103:               \\\\\\\"855f47de669d2f042839efded7e2468e102091b55097f3e403a24a0e3485b860\\\\\\\",\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:105:               \\\\\\\"4724a9da8856cf081c9f31c7a6974458829124f77bb19a7fd7790b80a567c206\\\\\\\",\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:107:               \\\\\\\"37f27bdb7036ae9907b231aade8401ab50f81b4061db16c926ba70fe12067972\\\\\\\",\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:109:               \\\\\\\"a569e60fdf199334f96147bf1d4b1ed028a4b99f70a9f85c88eb3871a47b7a58\\\\\\\"}},\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:127:               \\\\\\\"a29f3f8cceb6ec2b76185bf563d0e186bfb3d2f39cad2129c57dc31603bdb23b\\\\\\\",\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:129:               \\\\\\\"e3b11f882d64f64d843d8ab10a133a09cb076452dc3397a6c1fc708c3c026bd5\\\\\\\",\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:131:               \\\\\\\"fa00271187b2ba976be61c81acd3309b9ea8d19d458ce4a13a5ba1ed971ccf70\\\\\\\",\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:133:               \\\\\\\"e4c4693a640e53b971d37088809c74539cc0f1dda7d9c6a3cdd9beb7ce40a87d\\\\\\\"}}\\\"}]}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"10422e07-bed5-4d49-b8ad-810ba98736ef\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The JS/Dart digests are identical to the base ✓. The Java digest is identical ✓ (confirmed earlier). cst.jolt: layout must be 2 (:123), decode `:type` with error (:140), platform nil guard (:37) — os is nil → \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" → not \\\\\\\"mac\\\\\\\" → linux; arch is nil → x86_64. G1 has been addressed (with the caveat that nil is read as linux; no problem on macOS hosts where os.name is set; on a nil-shim Linux host it becomes linux, which is correct as per the udp_probe comment). The rationale is also documented. Good.\\\\n\\\\nNext is the parity harness — an important adversarial target. Going to read all of jolt_j0c_parity.clj.\\\\n\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Now the E1 harness itself, the key adversarial target.\\\\n\\\\n\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_f21af1df565c42a58da3362e\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_file\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"file_path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj\\\"}}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"model\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792496954432,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"de6e5f0b-8381-4f4e-bdfa-c7ae5e845b7f\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_f21af1df565c42a58da3362e\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Read 1/1 file, 339 lines\\\\n1: ;; J0c rounds 2-3: the jolt yang.antlr.cst adapter against the JVM oracle\\\\n2: ;; (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.8), under the Architect's\\\\n3: ;; equivalence E1 (collab/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity...).\\\\n4: ;;\\\\n5: ;;   . build/tools/jolt-env.sh\\\\n6: ;;   \\\\\\\"$JOLT_HOME\\\\\\\"/bin/jolt -Srepro \\\\\\\\\\\\n7: ;;     -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\\\\\\\"]}}}' \\\\\\\\\\\\n8: ;;     -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj\\\\n9: ;;\\\\n10: ;; For every jolt_j0c_corpus.edn entry, the packet from the jolt adapter\\\\n11: ;; (src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt, the ANTLR4 C++ parser marshalled into the\\\\n12: ;; shared yang.antlr.export) passes packet/validate! (source-aware) and meets\\\\n13: ;; E1 against the JVM oracle:\\\\n14: ;;\\\\n15: ;;   strict   every field of an ok packet; outcome, version, unit, grammar\\\\n16: ;;            and completeness of an error packet; cst/first-failure; every\\\\n17: ;;            probe answer and its cst/first-failure call sequence\\\\n18: ;;   errors   at least one; the FIRST error's :kind and :span equal the\\\\n19: ;;            JVM's; its :line/:column equal the JVM's when the offending\\\\n20: ;;            token is a real source token, and equal the pinned jolt\\\\n21: ;;            constant below when it is helper-fabricated (INDENT, DEDENT,\\\\n22: ;;            EOF, a zero-width NEWLINE); its :message is a non-empty string\\\\n23: ;;   exempt   message text and expecting-sets, and errors after the first\\\\n24: ;;\\\\n25: ;; Strict packet equality is still reported, as RECORD strict-diff lines, so\\\\n26: ;; nothing E1 exempts is hidden. For every probe text,\\\\n27: ;; yang.python.antlr.repl-probe/probe runs under jolt and must answer as on the\\\\n28: ;; JVM, making the same cst/first-failure calls with the same results.\\\\n29: ;;\\\\n30: ;; repl-probe requires yang.python.antlr.parser, whose :clj branch imports the\\\\n31: ;; generated Java recognizers; the command above therefore puts a stand-in\\\\n32: ;; yang.python.antlr.parser (src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub, holding only\\\\n33: ;; `make-worker`, the probe's one use of it, and the grammar id) on a root\\\\n34: ;; before the :jolt roots. The probe's own code is the tracked source.\\\\n35: ;;\\\\n36: ;; Prints PASS/DIVERGES per entry with a minimal diff, RECORD lines for the\\\\n37: ;; observations, and exits nonzero on any divergence or failure.\\\\n38: (ns jolt-j0c-parity\\\\n39:   (:require\\\\n40:     [clojure.edn :as edn]\\\\n41:     [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\\\\n42: \\\\n43: \\\\n44: (def ^:private before (set (loaded-libs)))\\\\n45: \\\\n46: \\\\n47: (require '[yang.antlr.cst :as cst] '[yang.antlr.export :as export]\\\\n48:          '[yang.antlr.packet :as packet])\\\\n49: \\\\n50: \\\\n51: (def ^:private added (sort (remove before (loaded-libs))))\\\\n52: \\\\n53: (def corpus-path \\\\\\\"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn\\\\\\\")\\\\n54: (def oracle-path \\\\\\\"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn\\\\\\\")\\\\n55: \\\\n56: (def diverged (atom []))\\\\n57: (def failed (atom 0))\\\\n58: \\\\n59: \\\\n60: (defn- check\\\\n61:   [label ok? detail]\\\\n62:   (when-not ok? (swap! failed inc))\\\\n63:   (println (if ok? \\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"FAIL\\\\\\\") label (pr-str detail)))\\\\n64: \\\\n65: \\\\n66: (check \\\\\\\"yang.antlr.cst resolves to the jolt adapter (export-records exists) and loads only export/packet beside it\\\\\\\"\\\\n67:        (and (some? (resolve 'yang.antlr.cst/export-records))\\\\n68:             (= '(yang.antlr.cst yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet) added))\\\\n69:        added)\\\\n70: \\\\n71: \\\\n72: ;; The grammar id the JVM profile carries, read from its one definition.\\\\n73: (def grammar-id\\\\n74:   (let [src (slurp \\\\\\\"src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc\\\\\\\")\\\\n75:         [_ s] (re-find #\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\(def grammar-id\\\\\\\\s+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"[^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\s+(\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"[^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\)\\\\\\\" src)]\\\\n76:     (edn/read-string s)))\\\\n77: \\\\n78: \\\\n79: (def profile {:grammar-id grammar-id})\\\\n80: (def corpus (edn/read-string (slurp corpus-path)))\\\\n81: (def oracle (edn/read-string (slurp oracle-path)))\\\\n82: \\\\n83: \\\\n84: (check \\\\\\\"oracle is the JVM's, for this grammar id, and covers the corpus in order\\\\\\\"\\\\n85:        (and (= :jvm (:host oracle))\\\\n86:             (= grammar-id (:grammar-id oracle))\\\\n87:             (= (mapv (juxt :id :source) (:entries corpus))\\\\n88:                (mapv (juxt :id :source) (:entries oracle)))\\\\n89:             (= (:probe corpus) (mapv :text (:probe oracle))))\\\\n90:        {:host (:host oracle), :grammar-id grammar-id})\\\\n91: \\\\n92: \\\\n93: ;; -- minimal diffs -------------------------------------------------------------\\\\n94: \\\\n95: (defn- map-diff\\\\n96:   [a b]\\\\n97:   (into {}\\\\n98:         (keep (fn [k]\\\\n99:                 (when (not= (get a k) (get b k))\\\\n100:                   [k {:jvm (get a k), :jolt (get b k)}])))\\\\n101:         (distinct (concat (keys a) (keys b)))))\\\\n102: \\\\n103: \\\\n104: (defn- seq-diff\\\\n105:   \\\\\\\"Index-wise differences of two record vectors, at most `limit` of them.\\\\\\\"\\\\n106:   [xs ys limit]\\\\n107:   (let [n (max (count xs) (count ys))]\\\\n108:     (->> (range n)\\\\n109:          (keep (fn [i]\\\\n110:                  (let [x (get xs i) y (get ys i)]\\\\n111:                    (when (not= x y)\\\\n112:                      [i (if (and (map? x) (map? y)) (map-diff x y) {:jvm x, :jolt y})]))))\\\\n113:          (take limit)\\\\n114:          vec)))\\\\n115: \\\\n116: \\\\n117: (defn- packet-diff\\\\n118:   [jvm jolt]\\\\n119:   (let [top (dissoc (map-diff (dissoc jvm :yang.cst/nodes :yang.cst/errors)\\\\n120:                               (dissoc jolt :yang.cst/nodes :yang.cst/errors)))]\\\\n121:     (cond-> {}\\\\n122:       (seq top) (assoc :fields top)\\\\n123:       (not= (:yang.cst/nodes jvm) (:yang.cst/nodes jolt))\\\\n124:       (assoc :nodes {:count [(count (:yang.cst/nodes jvm)) (count (:yang.cst/nodes jolt))],\\\\n125:                      :first (seq-diff (vec (:yang.cst/nodes jvm))\\\\n126:                                       (vec (:yang.cst/nodes jolt)) 4)})\\\\n127:       (not= (:yang.cst/errors jvm) (:yang.cst/errors jolt))\\\\n128:       (assoc :errors (seq-diff (vec (:yang.cst/errors jvm))\\\\n129:                                (vec (:yang.cst/errors jolt)) 8)))))\\\\n130: \\\\n131: \\\\n132: ;; -- the corpus ----------------------------------------------------------------\\\\n133: \\\\n134: (defn- alternative-failure\\\\n135:   \\\\\\\"The JS/Dart rule (the listener's offending token even for no-viable-alt),\\\\n136:    as an observation of the C++ token buffer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n137:   [source]\\\\n138:   (let [{:keys [failure tokens]} (cst/export-records source)\\\\n139:         {:keys [kind index]} failure]\\\\n140:     (export/failure-kind\\\\n141:       (case kind 0 nil 1 :lexical 2 index)\\\\n142:       (keep (fn [{:keys [type index start stop]}]\\\\n143:               (when (not= -1 type) {:index index, :start start, :stop stop}))\\\\n144:             tokens))))\\\\n145: \\\\n146: \\\\n147: (def helper-pins\\\\n148:   \\\\\\\"The jolt host's [line column] for each entry whose first error is on a\\\\n149:    helper-fabricated token (macOS arm64, the round-3 :cpp helper patch). They\\\\n150:    equal the JVM's because that patch gives the C++ helper the Java helper's\\\\n151:    coordinates (lexer line/column for INDENT and EOF, last token's line for\\\\n152:    DEDENT); a drift in any of them fails the run.\\\\\\\"\\\\n153:   {:blank-lines [3 2],                  ; INDENT on line 3 after its spaces\\\\n154:    :missing-final-newline [1 5],        ; EOF at the end of line 1\\\\n155:    :malformed-unexpected-indent [1 2],  ; INDENT on line 1 after its spaces\\\\n156:    :malformed-unexpected-dedent [2 0],  ; DEDENT takes the last token's line\\\\n157:    :incomplete-def-header [2 0],        ; EOF at the start of line 2\\\\n158:    :incomplete-decorator [2 0],         ; EOF at the start of line 2\\\\n159:    :incomplete-continuation [2 0],      ; EOF at the start of line 2\\\\n160:    :bracket-list-open [3 0],            ; EOF at the start of line 3\\\\n161:    :bracket-dict-open [2 0]})           ; EOF at the start of line 2\\\\n162: \\\\n163: \\\\n164: (defn- helper-token?\\\\n165:   \\\\\\\"Whether the first error's offending token was fabricated by the lexer\\\\n166:    helper rather than read from the source.\\\\\\\"\\\\n167:   [records]\\\\n168:   (let [{:keys [type a b]} (first (:errors records))\\\\n169:         tn (cond (= -1 type) \\\\\\\"EOF\\\\\\\"\\\\n170:                  (< -1 type (count (:symbolic records))) ((:symbolic records) type))]\\\\n171:     (boolean (or (#{\\\\\\\"INDENT\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"DEDENT\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"EOF\\\\\\\"} tn)\\\\n172:                  (and (= \\\\\\\"NEWLINE\\\\\\\" tn) (< b a))))))\\\\n173: \\\\n174: \\\\n175: (defn- e1-violations\\\\n176:   \\\\\\\"E1 for one entry; [] when it holds.\\\\\\\"\\\\n177:   [id source jvm jolt records]\\\\n178:   (let [bad (volatile! [])\\\\n179:         note! (fn [why detail] (vswap! bad conj [why detail]))]\\\\n180:     (when-let [v (try (packet/validate! jolt {:source source}) nil\\\\n181:                       (catch Throwable e (ex-data e)))]\\\\n182:       (note! :validate! v))\\\\n183:     (if (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome jvm))\\\\n184:       (when-not (= jvm jolt) (note! :ok-packet-not-equal (packet-diff jvm jolt)))\\\\n185:       (let [base (fn [p] (dissoc p :yang.cst/errors))\\\\n186:             [je] (:yang.cst/errors jvm)\\\\n187:             [ke] (:yang.cst/errors jolt)\\\\n188:             helper? (helper-token? records)\\\\n189:             pin (get helper-pins id)]\\\\n190:         (when-not (= (base jvm) (base jolt))\\\\n191:           (note! :packet-fields (map-diff (base jvm) (base jolt))))\\\\n192:         (when-not (seq (:yang.cst/errors jolt)) (note! :no-errors nil))\\\\n193:         (when-not (= (:kind je) (:kind ke)) (note! :first-kind [(:kind je) (:kind ke)]))\\\\n194:         (when-not (= (:span je) (:span ke)) (note! :first-span [(:span je) (:span ke)]))\\\\n195:         (when-not (and (string? (:message ke)) (seq (:message ke)))\\\\n196:           (note! :first-message (:message ke)))\\\\n197:         (cond\\\\n198:           (and helper? (nil? pin)) (note! :helper-token-unpinned [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\\\n199:           (and (not helper?) pin) (note! :pin-on-a-real-token pin)\\\\n200:           helper? (when-not (= pin [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\\\n201:                     (note! :helper-pin-drift {:pin pin, :jolt [(:line ke) (:column ke)]}))\\\\n202:           :else (when-not (= [(:line je) (:column je)] [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\\\n203:                   (note! :first-line-column {:jvm [(:line je) (:column je)],\\\\n204:                                              :jolt [(:line ke) (:column ke)]})))))\\\\n205:     @bad))\\\\n206: \\\\n207: \\\\n208: (def results (atom []))\\\\n209: \\\\n210: \\\\n211: (doseq [{:keys [id source packet first-failure]} (:entries oracle)]\\\\n212:   (let [worker (cst/make-worker profile)\\\\n213:         jolt-packet (try (cst/parse-unit worker [:unit 0] source)\\\\n214:                          (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-message e), :data (ex-data e)}))\\\\n215:         jolt-ff (try (cst/first-failure worker source)\\\\n216:                      (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-message e)}))\\\\n217:         records (cst/export-records source)\\\\n218:         strict (= packet jolt-packet)\\\\n219:         e1 (if (:thrown jolt-packet)\\\\n220:              [[:thrown jolt-packet]]\\\\n221:              (e1-violations id source packet jolt-packet records))\\\\n222:         ff-ok (= first-failure jolt-ff)\\\\n223:         ok (and (empty? e1) ff-ok)\\\\n224:         alt (alternative-failure source)]\\\\n225:     (swap! results conj {:id id, :strict strict, :e1 (empty? e1), :first-failure ff-ok})\\\\n226:     (when-not ok (swap! diverged conj id))\\\\n227:     (println (if ok \\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"FAIL-E1\\\\\\\") id\\\\n228:              (pr-str (cond-> {:outcome (:yang.cst/outcome packet),\\\\n229:                               :nodes (count (:yang.cst/nodes packet)),\\\\n230:                               :first-failure first-failure,\\\\n231:                               :strict strict}\\\\n232:                        (seq e1) (assoc :e1-violations e1)\\\\n233:                        (not ff-ok) (assoc :jolt-first-failure jolt-ff))))\\\\n234:     (when-not (or strict (:thrown jolt-packet))\\\\n235:       (println \\\\\\\"RECORD strict-diff\\\\\\\" id (pr-str (packet-diff packet jolt-packet))))\\\\n236:     (println \\\\\\\"RECORD token-buffer\\\\\\\" id\\\\n237:              (pr-str {:first-error (:failure records),\\\\n238:                       :buffered-after (count (:tokens records)),\\\\n239:                       :offending-token-rule alt,\\\\n240:                       :rules-agree (= alt jolt-ff)}))))\\\\n241: \\\\n242: \\\\n243: (check \\\\\\\"every helper pin names a corpus entry\\\\\\\"\\\\n244:        (every? (set (map :id (:entries oracle))) (keys helper-pins))\\\\n245:        (remove (set (map :id (:entries oracle))) (keys helper-pins)))\\\\n246: \\\\n247: \\\\n248: ;; -- E1 refuses what it must (the checker is not vacuous) ------------------------\\\\n249: \\\\n250: (let [entry (fn [id] (first (filter #(= id (:id %)) (:entries oracle))))\\\\n251:       run (fn [id f]\\\\n252:             (let [{:keys [source packet]} (entry id)\\\\n253:                   jolt (f (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0] source))]\\\\n254:               (set (map first (e1-violations id source packet jolt\\\\n255:                                              (cst/export-records source))))))\\\\n256:       first-error (fn [k f] #(update-in % [:yang.cst/errors 0 k] f))]\\\\n257:   (doseq [[label id f expect]\\\\n258:           [[\\\\\\\"an INDENT at line 0 (the round-2 C++ helper)\\\\\\\" :malformed-unexpected-indent\\\\n259:             (first-error :line (constantly 0)) #{:validate! :helper-pin-drift}]\\\\n260:            [\\\\\\\"a helper-token column off its pin\\\\\\\" :incomplete-decorator\\\\n261:             (first-error :column inc) #{:helper-pin-drift}]\\\\n262:            [\\\\\\\"a real-token line off the JVM's\\\\\\\" :malformed-def\\\\n263:             (first-error :line inc) #{:first-line-column}]\\\\n264:            [\\\\\\\"a first-error span off the JVM's\\\\\\\" :malformed-def\\\\n265:             (first-error :span (fn [[a b]] [a (inc b)])) #{:first-span}]\\\\n266:            [\\\\\\\"an empty message\\\\\\\" :malformed-def\\\\n267:             (first-error :message (constantly \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")) #{:first-message}]\\\\n268:            [\\\\\\\"no errors\\\\\\\" :malformed-def\\\\n269:             #(assoc % :yang.cst/errors []) #{:no-errors}]\\\\n270:            [\\\\\\\"an ok packet with one node changed\\\\\\\" :arith-add\\\\n271:             #(assoc-in % [:yang.cst/nodes 1 :rule] \\\\\\\"expr\\\\\\\") #{:ok-packet-not-equal}]]]\\\\n272:     (let [got (run id f)]\\\\n273:       (check (str \\\\\\\"[E1 self-test] refuses \\\\\\\" label)\\\\n274:              (every? got expect)\\\\n275:              got))))\\\\n276: \\\\n277: \\\\n278: ;; -- a failed native parse never yields a packet --------------------------------\\\\n279: \\\\n280: (ffi/defcfn c-inject-fault \\\\\\\"jolt_parser_inject_fault\\\\\\\" [:int] :int)\\\\n281: \\\\n282: \\\\n283: (doseq [mode [1 2 3]]\\\\n284:   (c-inject-fault mode)\\\\n285:   (let [r (try (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0] \\\\\\\"1 + 2\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\")\\\\n286:                (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-data e)})\\\\n287:                (finally (c-inject-fault 0)))]\\\\n288:     (check (str \\\\\\\"[injected mode \\\\\\\" mode \\\\\\\"] parse-unit throws the wrapper status, no packet\\\\\\\")\\\\n289:            (and (contains? r :thrown) (#{-2 -3} (get-in r [:thrown :status])))\\\\n290:            r)))\\\\n291: \\\\n292: \\\\n293: (check \\\\\\\"after the injected failures parse-unit answers the oracle packet again\\\\\\\"\\\\n294:        (= (:packet (first (:entries oracle)))\\\\n295:           (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0]\\\\n296:                           (:source (first (:entries oracle)))))\\\\n297:        (:id (first (:entries oracle))))\\\\n298: \\\\n299: \\\\n300: ;; -- the completeness probe ----------------------------------------------------\\\\n301: \\\\n302: (require '[yang.python.antlr.repl-probe :as probe])\\\\n303: \\\\n304: \\\\n305: (check \\\\\\\"repl-probe's parser is the stand-in (no rule-names: the tracked parser.cljc did not load) with the same grammar id\\\\\\\"\\\\n306:        (and (nil? (resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/rule-names))\\\\n307:             (= grammar-id @(resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/grammar-id)))\\\\n308:        @(resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/grammar-id))\\\\n309: \\\\n310: \\\\n311: (doseq [{:keys [text first-failure-calls], answer :probe} (:probe oracle)]\\\\n312:   (let [calls (volatile! [])\\\\n313:         ff cst/first-failure\\\\n314:         jolt-answer (with-redefs [cst/first-failure\\\\n315:                                   (fn [w s] (let [r (ff w s)] (vswap! calls conj [s r]) r))]\\\\n316:                       (probe/probe text))\\\\n317:         ok (and (= answer jolt-answer) (= first-failure-calls @calls))]\\\\n318:     (when-not ok (swap! diverged conj [:probe text]))\\\\n319:     (println (if ok \\\\\\\"PASS\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"FAIL\\\\\\\") \\\\\\\"probe\\\\\\\" (pr-str text)\\\\n320:              (pr-str (cond-> {:probe answer, :calls first-failure-calls}\\\\n321:                        (not ok) (assoc :jolt-probe jolt-answer, :jolt-calls @calls))))))\\\\n322: \\\\n323: \\\\n324: (let [rs @results]\\\\n325:   (println \\\\\\\"RECORD totals\\\\\\\"\\\\n326:            (pr-str {:entries (count rs),\\\\n327:                     :e1 (count (filter :e1 rs)),\\\\n328:                     :strict-packets-equal (count (filter :strict rs)),\\\\n329:                     :first-failure-equal (count (filter :first-failure rs)),\\\\n330:                     :probe-texts (count (:probe oracle)),\\\\n331:                     :diverged @diverged})))\\\\n332: \\\\n333: \\\\n334: (println \\\\\\\"SUMMARY\\\\\\\" (if (and (empty? @diverged) (zero? @failed))\\\\n335:                      \\\\\\\"ok\\\\\\\"\\\\n336:                      (str (count @diverged) \\\\\\\" diverged, \\\\\\\" @failed \\\\\\\" failed\\\\\\\")))\\\\n337: \\\\n338: \\\\n339: (System/exit (if (and (empty? @diverged) (zero? @failed)) 0 1))\\\"}]}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"4fee08f7-31bc-48dc-9bbd-75795832bbf0\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Adversarial analysis of the E1 harness:\\\\n\\\\nFidelity to the ruling:\\\\n- Ruling E1: strict comparison of ok-packet fields (:outcome :complete :nodes :root), first-failure, and probe responses + call sequences. Harness: ok packet → full packet equality `(= jvm jolt)` (stronger than the enumerated fields, no problem). Error packet → base fields excluding :yang.cst/errors are strictly compared (including outcome, version, unit, grammar, complete — everything except errors). ✓\\\\n- Error packet: validate! with source ✓; ≥1 error ✓; :kind and :span of the first error equal ✓; message is a non-empty string ✓; line/column: real tokens equal to JVM, helper-generated ones equal to fixed pins ✓. Helper-generated ones defined as INDENT/DEDENT/EOF/zero-width NEWLINE — the ruling says \\\\\\\"INDENT, DEDENT, synthetic NEWLINE, EOF\\\\\\\". Harness's helper-token? uses jolt's own problematic-token-type record (from the record's :type), with zero-width NEWLINE detection as `(and (= \\\\\\\"NEWLINE\\\\\\\" tn) (< b a))` — using the span's a/b from the error record. Matches the ruling.\\\\n- Exemptions: message body, expecting set, errors from the first onward ✓.\\\\n\\\\nIs there any way the harness could pass while the packet is still wrong?\\\\n- Errors from the first onward are exempted by the ruling — could they hide a wrong error count? Yes. But this is the E1 the ruling acknowledged (consumers only read the first one). The strict-diff RECORD line keeps them visible (3 entries recorded). The ruling states that count is not a consumer concern. OK.\\\\n- Node comparison on error packets: the ruling says node strictness only for ok packets. The harness exempts nodes on error packets! Wait — ruling E1: \\\\\\\"strict: :outcome, :complete, :nodes, :root of ok packets\\\\\\\". So nodes on error packets are not subject to strict comparison in the ruling either. The harness compares base fields (dissoc errors) — including :yang.cst/nodes! `(base jvm) vs (base jolt)` — since base only dissocs :yang.cst/errors, nodes are strictly compared even on error packets. Stronger than the ruling. Good (no weakening).\\\\n- validate! catches exceptions and records ex-data as a violation ✓. If validate! returns without throwing but the packet is invalid… validate! is the shared contract.\\\\n- Thrown jolt packets are counted as violations [[:thrown ...]] → FAIL-E1 ✓.\\\\n- ff-ok is a strict comparison ✓.\\\\n- Pins: helper-token-unpinned is a violation; pins for real-token entries are violations; pins not in the corpus are violations (check :243-245). So the pin set cannot be silently expanded; adding a pin to an entry whose first error is a real token will fail. Can the harness be gamed by pinning everything? No — pin-on-a-real-token will fail. Can a genuine line/column mismatch slip through via a pin? Only if the problematic token is helper-generated, and only if the pin matches jolt's (wrong) value — but the pins equal JVM values (the report states all 9 equal JVM; verifiable from PASS + strict diff records: entries 1, 2, 3's lines are now strictly equal — meaning the pin values equal JVM values). Actually, is pin == JVM enforced? For helper tokens, the harness compares the pin to JOLT's line/column, not JVM's. If the pin had been set to a wrong jolt value different from JVM, E1 would still pass — that is by design the very purpose of per-host pinning (the ruling permits per-host constants). But the ruling says \\\\\\\"because the patch gives the C++ helper Java's coordinates, everything now equals JVM\\\\\\\" — the report claims equivalence; the RECORD strict-diff shows the remaining diffs are message-only for these entries, corroborating that line/column equal JVM. Honest.\\\\n- Self-test: 7 mutated packets, each expecting specific violation reasons; `(every? got expect)` — checks that all expected reasons are present. Note: run computes e1-violations on the mutated packet, but `records` is obtained from the actual parse (unmutated) — helper-token? uses the actual record; appropriate. The mutation set covers the checker's branches: validate!+pin-drift, pin-drift, first-line-column, first-span, first-message, no-errors, ok-packet-not-equal. Are these genuine tests of the checker: yes, they mutate real jolt packets and assert that the checker flags them for the right reasons. Not vacuous. One gap: no self-test for :helper-token-unpinned and :pin-on-a-real-token (the pin-discipline branches). Minor — could mention it. The self-test for \\\\\\\"INDENT at line 0\\\\\\\" expects #{:validate! :helper-pin-drift} and got must contain both — good (also verifies the round-2 helper's value violates the packet contract, corroborating the architect's (d)).\\\\n\\\\nHonesty of the pins: 9 pins discovered via the discovery path (r3-discover.log), the reason each entry's first error is a helper token is documented in one line; the NEWLINE first-error entry (malformed-unexpected-newline) has width → real → checked against JVM. Pin values are reasonable (INDENT after a space is line 3 col 2, \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\", etc.). Tuned to pass? The pins equal JVM values, and drift fails; discovery was type-driven, not outcome-driven. Honest.\\\\n\\\\nPins are in the harness rather than the fixture (the ruling says fixture): deviation disclosed by the author (report line 29), per the brief's instruction. Acceptable? The ruling's intent is that pins are tracked, reviewed, and drift causes failure — the harness is a tracked source file; but the fixture would bind pins to oracle regeneration. The brief explicitly instructed the harness. Flag as an accepted deviation, recommend moving to fixture at J5 conformance (the ruling's J5 item states \\\\\\\"jolt pins via conformance/check-error\\\\\\\"). Info level.\\\\n\\\\n(b) Helper patch: exact-text mechanism, uniqueness enforced, digest re-pinned (65027edf → 2483e344), the first regeneration fails closed (exit 1, r3-gen-cpp-1.log), Java/JS/Dart digests are byte-identical (verified via grep: bb61a463/1c8ac0a2/8ba1f239/33274579; JS 855f47de/4724a9da/37f27bdb/a569e60f; Dart a29f3f8c/e3b11f88/fa002711/e4c4693a — all equal to base). DEDENT guard: `if (lastToken) dedent->setLine(lastToken->getLine());` — Java dereferences unconditionally; the guard prevents null dereference (DEDENT without a preceding token cannot naturally occur; the guard is disclosed in the manifest comment :210-212). Sound and disclosed. commonToken patch: replaces `lastToken ? lastToken->getLine() : 0, ...` with `getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` — matches Java's lexer-derived coordinates. One question: old-text uniqueness — the old line for commonToken includes `type, text`, which is distinguishable from positionedToken's `type, \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"` inserted earlier — and patches apply in order, with apply-patch requiring exactly one match; regeneration exit 0 proves application. ✓\\\\n\\\\n(c) Layout v2: JPX1 version 1→2 (:289 o.u32(2)), adds i32 offending-token type (-2 if none) to error records; cst.jolt rejects non-2 layouts (:123), decodes :type (:140). JPT1 round-1 entry unchanged — confirmed by reading: jolt_parser_parse region is untouched (the earlier grep showed only JPX1 modifications; round-1 probe 11 PASS re-run r3-probe-r1.log). Containment unchanged: export entry has the same try/catch, message() noexcept unchanged, fault seam shared. cst.jolt's bounds/topology validation unchanged except for version. Backward compat: nothing outside cst.jolt consumes JPX1 v1 (the round-2 harness used cst.jolt); version bump + strict rejection is the right choice. ✓\\\\n\\\\n(d) G1: `(or (System/getProperty \\\\\\\"os.name\\\\\\\") \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")` — nil → \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" → linux-x86_64; documented; keeps the load closure small (does not require jolt.socket.native — the load-closure assertion requires only cst+export+packet; adding a native require would break the assertion). Reasonable resolution; untested on an actually-nil host (disclosed). ✓ closed (macOS side), Linux-side test deferred and disclosed.\\\\n\\\\n(e) Report-to-log correspondence: r3-parity-final.log should show 106 PASS. Pass count: 51 entries + 40 probes + checks (adapter load 1, oracle 1, pin names 1, self-test 7, injected 3, re-parse 1) = 51+40+14 = 105… plus? Hmm 106. The entry PASS lines output by the entry loop = 51 (all pass), probe 40, standalone checks: 1 (adapter) + 1 (oracle) + 1 (pin) + 7 (self-test) + 3 (injected) + 1 (after injected) = 14. 51+40+14=105. The report says 106 PASS. Off by one — perhaps there's one more check somewhere… \\\\\\\"e1 51\\\\\\\" RECORD totals. Should verify the log's SUMMARY and count. Let me grep the r3-parity-final.log for the totals and count the PASS lines. Should verify before flagging. Also verify that the oracle fixture is unmodified — cannot hash; the orchestrator claims sha256 5aee6d10 is identical to HEAD; matches the round-2 value I have on hand. ✓\\\\n\\\\nLet me grep the log.\\\\n\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Let me verify the final parity log totals against the report's 106 PASS claim.\\\\n\\\\n\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_51ebc0ffeb5348229fa49fab\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"grep\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"RECORD totals|SUMMARY|FAIL\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r3-parity-final.log\\\",\\\"output_mode\\\":\\\"content\\\",\\\"-n\\\":true}},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_1a5c8b87d4024e8a8a94196c\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"grep\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"^PASS\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r3-parity-final.log\\\",\\\"output_mode\\\":\\\"count\\\"}}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"model\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792497042362,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"7e352b46-0a60-4e1d-9230-9738f418410d\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_51ebc0ffeb5348229fa49fab\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r3-parity-final.log:161:RECORD totals {:entries 51, :e1 51, :strict-packets-equal 48, :first-failure-equal 51, :probe-texts 40, :diverged []}\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r3-parity-final.log:162:SUMMARY ok\\\"}]},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_1a5c8b87d4024e8a8a94196c\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r3-parity-final.log:106\\\\n\\\\nFound 106 total occurrences across 1 file.\\\"}]}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"ce0f2310-834d-46d5-a0fb-8dfd2cd2f8da\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"106 PASS lines confirmed, SUMMARY ok, totals also match (51 e1, 48 strict, 51 ff, 40 probes, diverged is empty). My earlier count of 105 was off by one (probably an extra PASS somewhere — the log is the ground truth and matches the report).\\\\n\\\\nRemaining checks: strict-diff RECORD lines against the 3 exclusion entries — the report enumerates them; the log probably contains RECORD strict-diff lines. Also, whether the oracle fixtures have been modified — orchestrator's sha256 claim + re-run of r3-oracle. Forbidden zones: the manifest only has :cpp changes; parity/cst/cpp are within the scope of the jolt lane; haven't touched shared sources (report). src/jolt/README.md was already fixed in round 3? Per the brief, what changed in round 3 is: manifest, cpp, parity, cst.jolt — README was round 2. OK.\\\\n\\\\nWriting the review, within 600 words. Verdict: SIGNED OFF. Findings:\\\\n\\\\n- H1 info: pins are on the harness side rather than in fixtures (the ruling said fixtures) — the deviation is disclosed and per instructions; when J5 conformance lands, move the pins to fixtures/conformance.\\\\n- H2 info: the self-tests don't cover the two pin-discipline branches (:helper-token-unpinned, :pin-on-a-real-token) — they are implemented and enforced in the real entries, but there's no mutation proving it; add 2 mutation cases.\\\\n- H3 info: jolt_parser_layout_version still returns 1 even though JPX1 is v2 (G2 remains; the adapter rejects layout≠2 so there's no practical hole).\\\\n- Possibly a note: helper-token? NEWLINE zero-width detection uses (< b a) from the record span — no problem.\\\\n\\\\nChecks (a)-(e) summary. Things that couldn't be verified: digests/hashes (oracle 5aee6d10, cpp 2483e344, dylib 186260ec), byte-identity of the regenerated Java/JS/Dart outputs (only equivalence of manifest digests + author's comparison log), the discovery path logs, Linux, owner confirmation regarding the §7.8 wording.\\\\n\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 11:47:32 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 18:47:32 +07\\\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\\\n# Review: J0c round 3 (E1 standard, C++ helper patch, JPX1 v2)\\\\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\\\\n\\\\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\\\\n\\\\n## Findings\\\\n\\\\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| H1 | info | src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj:147-161 | Pins live in the harness; the ruling said \\\\\\\"recorded in the fixture\\\\\\\". Deviation is brief-directed and disclosed (report:29). The harness compensates: unpinned helper-token entries fail, pins on real-token entries fail, pins naming no corpus entry fail (:198-199, :243-245). | Move the pins into the fixture/`conformance` when the J5 harness lands, per the ruling's J5 item. |\\\\n| H2 | info | jolt_j0c_parity.clj:257-275 | The 7 self-test mutations don't exercise the two pin-discipline branches (`:helper-token-unpinned`, `:pin-on-a-real-token`); they are live code but only mutation-proven for the other five reasons. | Add two mutations (delete a pin; pin a real-token entry). |\\\\n| H3 | info | jolt_parser.cpp:402-403 | `jolt_parser_layout_version` still answers 1 while JPX1 is v2 (G2 carried). No practical hole: cst.jolt:123 refuses any layout but 2. | Separate version entry when layouts next evolve. |\\\\n\\\\n## Checks\\\\n\\\\n- (a) **E1 faithfulness.** The harness matches or exceeds the ruling everywhere: ok packets require FULL equality (ruling lists four fields); error packets compare all non-`:yang.cst/errors` fields strictly - including `:yang.cst/nodes`, which the ruling exempts, so nothing was weakened; `validate!` is source-aware on every packet; first-error `:kind`/`:span` equality, non-empty message, real-token line/column vs JVM, helper-token line/column vs pin; message text and post-first errors exempt exactly as ruled, and still visible as `RECORD strict-diff` lines (3 entries), so nothing exempt is hidden. A thrown jolt packet is itself a violation (:219-221). **Could it pass while wrong?** Only within the ruling's own exemptions (post-first errors) - acknowledged consumer-backed carve-outs, with the strict diffs recorded. **Self-test is real:** it mutates actual jolt packets (including restoring the round-2 helper's line-0 INDENT, which must trip both `:validate!` and `:helper-pin-drift` - confirming the Architect's (d) packet-contract claim) and asserts the violation reasons, not just failure. **Pins honest:** the 9 entries were found by a type-driven discovery pass (INDENT/DEDENT/EOF first errors), each with a one-line reason; the sole NEWLINE first-error entry has width, stays real-token-checked; pins equal JVM values (corroborated: entries 1-3 are now strict-equal except message), and any drift fails. Not outcome-tuned.\\\\n- (b) **Helper patch.** Same exact-text mechanism as Java/JS/Dart: uniqueness-enforced, fail-closed (first regeneration against the old digest exited 1, `r3-gen-cpp-1.log`), new digest `2483e344…` recorded; the `commonToken` old-text stays unique after the positionedToken insertion (`type, text` vs `type, \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"`). The DEDENT `if (lastToken)` guard is sound (a DEDENT requires a prior INDENT, so it never fires naturally; it only prevents a null-deref crash) and disclosed in the manifest comment (:210-212). Java/JS/Dart `:generated` digests and patch blocks byte-identical to base - I verified all 12 digests against my round-1 reads.\\\\n- (c) **JPX1 v2.** Version bumped in-buffer (:289), error records gain i32 offending type (-2 none), cst.jolt refuses layout ≠ 2 and decodes `:type`; round-1 JPT1 entry untouched and its probe re-passes 11/11; containment (`noexcept` `message()`, catch-alls), fault seam, bounds/topology validation all unchanged from the versions I already signed off. No consumer of v1 remains.\\\\n- (d) **G1 closed** as recommended: `(or (System/getProperty \\\\\\\"os.name\\\\\\\") \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")` (cst.jolt:37), nil→linux/x86_64, rationale commented, load closure stays cst+export+packet (harness asserts it). Untested on a real nil-shim host - disclosed.\\\\n- (e) **Report vs logs:** r3-parity-final.log has exactly 106 PASS lines, `RECORD totals {:entries 51, :e1 51, :strict-packets-equal 48, :first-failure-equal 51, :probe-texts 40, :diverged []}`, `SUMMARY ok` - matching the report, which keeps strict equality at 48/51 visible rather than claiming 51. Forbidden zones untouched (manifest delta confined to `:cpp`; no shared export/packet/dao/yin.vm edits; oracle fixture unchanged at `5aee6d10…` per orchestrator and the JVM determinism re-run). NOT-done list honest: Linux unrun, G1 untested there, §7.8→E1 wording awaiting owner, Architect (e)2/(e)3 untouched, round-2 deferrals unchanged.\\\\n\\\\n## Could not verify\\\\n\\\\n- Hashes myself (no shell): oracle `5aee6d10…`, `Python3LexerBase.cpp` `2483e344…`, dylib `186260ec…`, and the regenerated Java/JS/Dart output bytes (manifest-digest equality plus the author's `git show` comparison log are the evidence).\\\\n- `r3-discover.log` contents and the throwaway discovery script.\\\\n- Linux behaviour of the G1 nil path; owner confirmation of the E1 wording amendment.\\\"}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"model\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792497102753,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"bba0e9de-37f9-4002-9a73-9b38b1a384cd\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 14:53:56 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 21:53:56 +07\\\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\\\n# Task: review J0c round 4 (worker isolation, stack/thread, input policy, leaks, hardening)\\\\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\\\\nImplementers:\\\\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-20 21:53:56 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \\\\\\\"cmd for review\\\\\\\"; author claude-opus-5-5\\\\n\\\\nREAD-ONLY review of the UNCOMMITTED round-4 change in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c (branch jolt-j0c, HEAD 522a7930): modified native/jolt-parser/CMakeLists.txt, jolt_parser.cpp, src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj, jolt_j0c_parity.clj, jolt_native.clj, jolt_parser_probe.clj, src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt; new native/jolt-parser/exports.darwin, exports.linux, sanitize/CMakeLists.txt, sanitize/harness.cpp, src/dev/jolt_j0c_{cycles,gc,sanitize,stack,stack_child,workers}.clj, test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_input_{corpus,oracle}.edn. Report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792500314000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r4.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md; spec: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md sections 7.3-7.8. The orchestrator re-ran on macOS: native build rc 0, parity SUMMARY ok (E1 51/51), round-1 probe 11 PASS, smoke rc 0; the release library exports exactly 7 symbols with no test hooks, the test build exports 12 including the fault-injection switch.\\\\nCheck: (A) worker isolation: does a worker really own its lexer/parser simulators and caches (read the C++), does close-worker dispose correctly, is the 2-workers-on-one-thread and 4-threads equality evidence meaningful, are the \\\\\\\"static caches do not grow\\\\\\\" counters real (the author says an old static-cache path DOES grow them as a control); the author flags that the runtime's lazily-filled ATN memo is shared across workers (0 to 99 states) and that the spec says mutable caches may not be shared: assess whether the evidence supports \\\\\\\"pure, locked memo, JVM shares it too\\\\\\\" but DO NOT decide the design question; (B) stack/thread: are the measured budgets (main thread ~10752 parens ok, 11264 SIGSEGV; jolt thread 544 ok, 576 SIGILL; unary prefix-minus super-linear, 93.5 s at depth 1024) from child processes so a crash cannot kill the harness, is the method sound, are any numbers in the report absent from the logs; the GC-during-long-parse claim (GC at 200 ms of a 36 s parse finished at 271 ms with unrelated-thread progress) is it credible; (C) input policy: embedded NUL, CR/CRLF, BOM, edge code points vs a JVM-written oracle (13/13); the BOM bug the author found (the C++ runtime strips a leading UTF-8 BOM, jolt returned a SUCCESS packet with every span off by one; the wrapper now decodes the source itself): is the fix correct and complete, is the oracle JVM-only, invalid UTF-8/over-64MiB/bad-arg fail closed with no packet; (D) leaks: 20,000 parse/free cycles flat, ASan+UBSan harness clean, macOS leaks tool 0 leaks: sound? (E) hardening: exported-symbols list, CMake option defaulting OFF for test hooks, nonallocating failure paths preserved, export layout version entry, Linux version script untested; (F) anything forbidden touched (shared dao.*/yin.vm source, the oracle fixtures or recorded digests), and is the report honest about what is not done (Linux; close-worker not wired into shared callers; J5 budget policy undecided). Under 700 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), what you could not verify.\\\"}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792508045098,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"51536567-e855-4647-8f08-af24513e5cd4\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"This is a big round. First I'll read the report, then read the main files. Budget: report + C++ (worker ownership, BOM fix) + harness files + logs. Let's start with the report first.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"I'll start with the round-4 report, then the C++ and harnesses.\\\\n\\\\n\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_df16d52e8e6b4f9a831b3ad6\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_file\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"file_path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792500314000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r4.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\\\"}}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"model\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792508075303,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"7878fe26-01bb-4bb3-8d64-a7444a4cda05\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_df16d52e8e6b4f9a831b3ad6\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Read 1/1 file, 289 lines\\\\n1: Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 14:21:25 GMT\\\\n2: Created-Local: 2026-10-20 21:21:25 +07\\\\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\\\\n4: Session-ID: 4f6a4fdd-c79b-44eb-97f2-1ce81ad82aa3\\\\n5: # Findings: J0c round 4 - worker isolation, thread/stack evidence, input policy, leak checks, native hardening\\\\n6: Role: Compiler & AST / Stream engineer\\\\n7: Implementers:\\\\n8: - Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 19:45:14 +07 | Status: done (round 4, macOS arm64) | Rationale: owner routing \\\\\\\"opus-5.5 for implementation, cmd for review\\\\\\\"\\\\n9: \\\\n10: **Worktree:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c` (branch `jolt-j0c`, base `522a7930`). Nothing is staged or committed.\\\\n11: **Logs:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r4-*`.\\\\n12: **Platform:** macOS arm64 only. Linux x86_64 is NOT RUN; the orchestrator runs it.\\\\n13: **File scope:** `native/jolt-parser/`, `src/jolt/`, `src/dev/jolt_*`, and two new fixture files beside the round-2 fixtures.\\\\n14: \\\\n15: ## Ladder\\\\n16: \\\\n17: | step | result | evidence |\\\\n18: |---|---|---|\\\\n19: | A. Worker isolation (§7.4) | **DONE**, one audit item for review (A.3) | `r4-workers-final.log`: 8 PASS, SUMMARY ok |\\\\n20: | B. Thread and stack, collection during a long parse (§7.5, §7.6) | **DONE**: measured, no bound enforced | `r4-stack.log` / `build/j0c/r4/stack-budget.edn`, `r4-gc.log` |\\\\n21: | C. Input policy | **DONE**; it found and fixed one real defect: the BOM | `r4-parity-final.log`: 132 PASS, SUMMARY ok |\\\\n22: | D. Leaks and lifetime | **DONE** (LSan is not available on macOS; `leaks` used instead) | `r4-cycles-20000.log`, `r4-sanitize.log` |\\\\n23: | E. Native hardening | **DONE** (the Linux version script is untested) | `r4-nm.log`, `r4-digests.log`, `r4-probe-r1-release.log` |\\\\n24: \\\\n25: **Still green:**\\\\n26: - `bb smoke:jolt`: SUMMARY ok.\\\\n27: - round-1 probe: 11 PASS on the test build; on the release build 7 PASS plus a SKIP message for the 4 fault checks.\\\\n28: - E1 parity: SUMMARY ok, `e1 51`, strict 48/51, first-failure 51/51, probes 40/40, plus the new input section.\\\\n29: \\\\n30: ## A. Worker isolation\\\\n31: \\\\n32: ### A.1 Mechanism\\\\n33: \\\\n34: The design fixes this, and it matches the JVM `cst.clj:43-71`.\\\\n35: \\\\n36: **Native side** (`jolt_parser.cpp`):\\\\n37: - `jolt_parser_worker_new` returns an opaque `Worker`. It owns fresh `std::vector<dfa::DFA>` for the lexer and the parser, built over the generated ATNs' decision states, plus two `PredictionContextCache`s.\\\\n38: - `jolt_parser_export(worker, …)` installs fresh simulators over the worker's caches before recognition, replacing the generated defaults that point at the static caches:\\\\n39:   - `lexer.setInterpreter(new LexerATNSimulator(&lexer, *atn, w->lexer_dfa, w->lexer_ctx))`;\\\\n40:   - the same for the parser.\\\\n41: - `Recognizer::setInterpreter` deletes the generated default (`runtime/src/Recognizer.cpp:88-91`), and the generated destructors delete the installed one. So the simulators live per call, and the caches live as long as the worker.\\\\n42: - `jolt_parser_worker_free` disposes the handle.\\\\n43: \\\\n44: **Guards:**\\\\n45: - A tag rejects a NULL or unrecognized handle with −1.\\\\n46: - A per-worker atomic busy flag refuses a concurrent second call with a new status, **−5**. It is fail-closed, enforcing \\\\\\\"a worker is used by one thread at a time\\\\\\\" (§7.4).\\\\n47: \\\\n48: **jolt side** (`cst.jolt`):\\\\n49: - `make-worker` holds the handle in an atom.\\\\n50: - New `close-worker` is idempotent: a `compare-and-set!` frees it once.\\\\n51: - A closed worker throws \\\\\\\"jolt parser worker is closed\\\\\\\".\\\\n52: - `export-records` now takes the worker, and `first-failure` uses it.\\\\n53: \\\\n54: **The design gap to settle:** `close-worker` is jolt-only; the other hosts' workers are collected. The parser stage's close/reset (§7.4: \\\\\\\"its parser stage owns it and disposes it at close/reset\\\\\\\") lives in shared code (`yang.stage`, `yang.python.antlr.parser`, `repl_probe`). That is outside this track's files, so **those callers do not call it yet**. `repl_probe` makes a temporary worker per probe call, and under jolt each one keeps its native caches until the process exits. Wiring disposal into the stage and the probe is J5/integration work. The stand-in parser used by the harness has the same leak.\\\\n55: \\\\n56: ### A.2 Tests (`src/dev/jolt_j0c_workers.clj`, 8 PASS)\\\\n57: \\\\n58: - **Isolated reference:** each of the 51 corpus sources is parsed with its own fresh worker.\\\\n59: - **Two workers on ONE thread:**\\\\n60:   - A runs forwards and B backwards, alternating calls, for 3 passes;\\\\n61:   - every packet equals the isolated run.\\\\n62: - **4 workers on 4 threads:**\\\\n63:   - they start together behind a gate, each does 3 passes (153 parses) in mixed orders;\\\\n64:   - every packet equals the isolated run.\\\\n65: - **No static growth.** The generated static caches were counted through the test seam `jolt_parser_static_cache_stats`:\\\\n66:   - after initialization: lexer DFA 0 states/0 edges/0 ctx; parser DFA 1 state (the precedence-DFA start state the constructor makes)/0 edges/0 ctx;\\\\n67:   - after all worker parses: **identical**.\\\\n68: - **Positive control 1:** worker A's own caches filled to lexer DFA 89 states/265 edges, parser DFA 143 states/254 edges, 213 parser ctx entries.\\\\n69: - **Positive control 2:** one parse through the round-1 `jolt_parser_parse`, which uses the static caches, grew them to lexer 33/64 and parser 57/60/105. So the counters are live.\\\\n70: - **Busy refusal:** a second call while a long parse runs on the same worker is refused with −5, and the long parse (1,000,002 records) succeeds.\\\\n71: - **Close:** a closed worker refuses parses, and a second close is a no-op.\\\\n72: - **Concurrency observed:** a call on another worker runs concurrently on the main thread meanwhile (from `build/j0c/r2/overlap.clj`, a throwaway probe).\\\\n73: \\\\n74: ### A.3 Static-state audit (generated C++, helpers, runtime 4.13.2)\\\\n75: \\\\n76: **Generated, mutable, per-grammar static (the caches §7.4 names):**\\\\n77: - `Python3Lexer.cpp:33-34` and `Python3Parser.cpp:29-30`: `decisionToDFA`, `sharedContextCache` inside `Python3{Lexer,Parser}StaticData`.\\\\n78: - Held in the anonymous-namespace globals `Python3Lexer.cpp:49` and `Python3Parser.cpp:43`, initialized once through `call_once` (`Python3Lexer.cpp:763`).\\\\n79: - Wired into the default simulators at `Python3Lexer.cpp:623` and `Python3Parser.cpp:667`.\\\\n80: - `ANTLR4_USE_THREAD_LOCAL_CACHE` is not defined, so these are process-wide, not thread-local.\\\\n81: - **Replaced per worker (above), and shown not to grow.**\\\\n82: \\\\n83: **Generated, immutable after init (shared):**\\\\n84: - the ATN, `serializedATN`, `ruleNames`, `vocabulary`, `literalNames`, `symbolicNames`.\\\\n85: \\\\n86: **Helpers:**\\\\n87: - `Python3LexerBase` keeps only per-instance state: `tokens`, `indents`, `opened`, `lastToken`.\\\\n88: - `getIndentationCount` is a static pure function.\\\\n89: - `onNewLine` builds its `std::regex` per call.\\\\n90: - `Python3ParserBase` has no state.\\\\n91: - **No static mutable state.**\\\\n92: \\\\n93: **Runtime statics:**\\\\n94: - `tree/xpath/XPathLexer.cpp:46` (thread_local XPath lexer data), `tree/xpath/XPath.cpp:123`, `tree/ParseTreeWalker.cpp:18`, and `Parser.cpp:47` (`BypassAltsAtnCache`). None of them is reached by `file_input` recognition or by the wrapper; they serve XPath, tree walking and parse-tree patterns.\\\\n95: - `ATN::_mutex/_stateMutex/_edgeMutex` (`ATN.h:127-129`) are locks only.\\\\n96: \\\\n97: **OPEN, for review:** the shared ATN's next-token memo.\\\\n98: - `ATN::nextTokens(ATNState*)` lazily fills `ATNState::_nextTokenWithinRule` under `ATN::_mutex` (`runtime/src/atn/ATN.cpp:40-49`). It lives in the shared, generated ATN.\\\\n99: - **It does grow during worker recognition:** 0 → 99 parser ATN states after the corpus. The test records this (RECORD line); it does not assert it.\\\\n100: - It is a deterministic memo of a pure function of the ATN alone (no input or worker state), set once per state under a lock, and never read before it is set.\\\\n101: - The JVM shares exactly the same memo: Java's `ATN.nextTokens(ATNState)` caches `s.nextTokenWithinRule`, and `cst.clj` shares the generated ATN across workers. The design cites `cst.clj` as the isolation reference, so I read §7.4 as accepting it and **did not stop A**.\\\\n102: - If the Architect reads \\\\\\\"mutable caches may not [be shared]\\\\\\\" literally, there are two options:\\\\n103:   - (a) fill the memo for every ATN state once, at library initialization (cheap, a few thousand states), which makes it immutable before any recognition;\\\\n104:   - (b) deserialize a private ATN per worker (memory per worker, no shared ATN state at all).\\\\n105: - **I chose neither.**\\\\n106: \\\\n107: ## B. Thread, stack, collection\\\\n108: \\\\n109: **Actual thread configuration,** read on the parsing thread itself through `jolt_parser_thread_info` (`pthread_get_stacksize_np`/`pthread_get_stackaddr_np`/`pthread_main_np`):\\\\n110: \\\\n111: | parsing thread | stack bytes | main thread | used at the call |\\\\n112: |---|---|---|---|\\\\n113: | jolt's script/main thread (`jolt -M:jolt file.clj`) | 10,027,008 | yes | 9,089 B |\\\\n114: | a thread jolt starts with `Thread.` | 536,576 | no | 241 B |\\\\n115: \\\\n116: - **The `:blocking` parse runs on the calling thread:** the reported stack is the caller's own.\\\\n117: - **The main thread's stack is RLIMIT_STACK:** this shell's `ulimit -s` is 9792 KiB = 10,027,008 bytes. So the main-thread budget is inherited from whoever launches jolt and is **not a property of the build**.\\\\n118: - **A jolt `Thread.` gets the macOS default 512 KiB pthread stack:** 536,576 bytes as reported. jolt sets no thread stack size that I could see.\\\\n119: \\\\n120: **Nesting budget** (`bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_stack.clj`):\\\\n121: - Every parse runs in a child jolt process, so a crash kills only the child.\\\\n122: - Per shape and per thread kind: doubling depths, then 16 steps inside the failing interval, then a FRESH child (cold caches) at the last passing depth through the full jolt path (native, decode, shared exporter).\\\\n123: - Native-only runs (`native` mode) crash at the same depths as full runs (parens: main passes 8,000 and dies before 16,000 in both modes; a jolt thread passes 500 and dies before 1,000), so **the overflow is the native recursion** (ANTLR's recursive descent and the wrapper's walk), not the jolt side.\\\\n124: \\\\n125: Final run: `r4-stack.log`, written to `build/j0c/r4/stack-budget.edn`, exit 0. The time cap is 20 s per parse. \\\\\\\"passes\\\\\\\" is the last depth that passed in the 16-step refinement; a fresh child (cold caches) then passed it again through the full jolt path.\\\\n126: \\\\n127: | shape | main thread (10,027,008 B): passes / dies at / exit / B per level | jolt `Thread.` (536,576 B): passes / dies at / exit / B per level |\\\\n128: |---|---|---|\\\\n129: | `(`…`)` parens | 10,752 / 11,264 / 139 SIGSEGV / 890 | 544 / 576 / 132 SIGILL / 931 |\\\\n130: | `[`…`]` list | 10,752 / 11,264 / 139 / 890 | 544 / 576 / 132 / 931 |\\\\n131: | `f(`…`)` call | 9,728 / 10,240 / 139 / 979 | 512 / 544 / 132 / 986 |\\\\n132: | unary `-`…`-1` | **time-limited before any crash:** 512 in 10.3 s, 1,024 in 93.5 s. Time grows about ×6–9 per doubling (cubic-like) | 640 passes (fresh full re-run 20.7 s). The doubling ladder crashed (exit 132) between 512 and 1,024; the refinement stopped at the time cap at 672, not on a crash. Treat this one as time-bound |\\\\n133: | `not not … 1` | no failure up to 65,536 (the ladder top) | 5,376 / 5,632 / 132 / 95 |\\\\n134: | `2 ** 2 ** …` | no failure up to 65,536 | 6,656 / 6,912 / 132 / 77 |\\\\n135: | `lambda: lambda: …` | 55,296 / 57,344 / 139 / 174 | 2,944 / 3,072 / 132 / 174 |\\\\n136: | `1 if 1 else …` (ternary) | no crash up to 65,536. Time is linear: 32,768 in 14.2 s, and 65,536 parsed fine in 28.5 s but went past the cap, which ended the ladder | 5,376 / 5,632 / 132 / 95 |\\\\n137: | nested `if` blocks (indentation) | no failure up to 8,192 (that ladder's top; the input grows quadratically) | 1,280 / 1,344 / 132 / 399 |\\\\n138: \\\\n139: **Comparison, the JVM oracle on its default main-thread stack** (`clojure -M -e`):\\\\n140: - parens nesting 256 parses; **512 throws `StackOverflowError`** (catchable on the JVM).\\\\n141: - unary takes 328 ms / 986 ms / 6,005 ms at 128/256/512, the same super-linear growth, so it is the grammar/ALL(*) cost and not the C++ runtime's.\\\\n142: - So the C++ main thread nests deeper than the JVM oracle can. Parity beyond the JVM's own limit has no oracle; J5 must decide the bound.\\\\n143: \\\\n144: **Failure mode at and above the bracket:**\\\\n145: - **Main thread:** the process dies with SIGSEGV (exit 139).\\\\n146:   - This holds for every crashing shape on both thread kinds; the jolt `Thread.` line below likewise.\\\\n147: - **jolt `Thread.`:** the process dies with SIGILL (exit 132).\\\\n148: - Either way nothing is reported, there is no exception, and no catch is possible, as §7.6 says. Every crash happened in a child, and the harness survived and recorded it.\\\\n149: \\\\n150: **No bound is enforced in `cst.jolt`;** §7.6 assigns enforcement to J5. **Budget policy is not chosen here.** Options for J5:\\\\n151: - per-thread-kind budgets from the measured `passes` with a safety factor (for example 1/2);\\\\n152: - run parses on an owned thread created with an explicit large stack, so the budget no longer depends on RLIMIT_STACK or jolt's default;\\\\n153: - a pre-parse nesting count, with the §7.6 caveat that brackets alone do not bound every shape (unary, `not`, `lambda`, ternary and `**` nest without brackets; see the table);\\\\n154: - **and a time/resource bound, not only a stack bound.** Prefix unary nesting reaches 93.5 s at depth 1,024, long before any overflow, on the JVM as well. No asynchronous cancellation of a native call exists (§7.6), so bounding the input is the only defence against this input.\\\\n155: \\\\n156: **Collection during a long parse** (`src/dev/jolt_j0c_gc.clj`, `r4-gc.log`):\\\\n157: \\\\n158: | | value |\\\\n159: |---|---|\\\\n160: | setup | one `jolt_parser_export` call (30,000 statements, 28,203,837-byte answer) on thread P, encoded before P starts; `jolt.host/gc-full!` on main 200 ms into the call; unrelated thread U ticks every ~5 ms |\\\\n161: | `:blocking` | call 0.1 → 35,826 ms; collection 206.8 → 271.1 ms (**completed during the call**); U: 5,237 ticks during the call |\\\\n162: | plain binding (recorded, not asserted) | collection 203.4 → 349.2 ms, also during the call; U: 4,830 ticks |\\\\n163: \\\\n164: - As J0a found, this does not distinguish the two modes on jolt; no claim rests on `:blocking` semantics.\\\\n165: \\\\n166: **Observation, not a performance claim:**\\\\n167: - Native parse time is linear, at about 0.9 ms per statement of `x = (1 + 2) * 3`: 1,000 → 880 ms, 8,000 → 7,728 ms.\\\\n168: - The JVM path (parse plus export) is about 0.48 ms per statement.\\\\n169: - Both were measured with the stack run loading the CPU.\\\\n170: - One cause seen in a profile: the Python3 lexer's start state carries a semantic predicate (`atStartOfInput()`), so DFA state 0 is never cached (`suppressEdge`), and every token runs `LexerATNSimulator::matchATN`. The JVM runtime behaves the same way.\\\\n171: \\\\n172: ## C. Input policy\\\\n173: \\\\n174: **Tracked fixtures:**\\\\n175: - corpus: `test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_input_corpus.edn`, 13 sources;\\\\n176: - oracle: `jolt_j0c_input_oracle.edn`, SHA-256 `954a479379c449247737432ee1c4b6c1ccc66f6d3c7331e45b39ec567658bbf0`. It is written only on the JVM by `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle input` and records each source's code points.\\\\n177: - The round-2 oracle is untouched: `5aee6d10…`, unchanged, and it regenerates identically.\\\\n178: \\\\n179: | case | policy | result |\\\\n180: |---|---|---|\\\\n181: | embedded NUL: in a string, in a comment, after a statement, alone, in indentation | parity with the JVM (E1) | 5/5 PASS, all strictly equal |\\\\n182: | empty, CRLF, lone CR, U+FFFD, U+10FFFF, U+FFFF, DEL | parity (E1) | 7/7 PASS, strictly equal |\\\\n183: | **BOM at start** `\\\\\\\"﻿x = 1\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"` | parity (E1) | **FAILED first, then fixed**: see below. PASS, strictly equal |\\\\n184: | invalid UTF-8 at the C ABI: lone 0xFF, truncated sequence, overlong `/`, encoded U+D800, above U+10FFFF, 0xFF in a string literal | no JVM equivalent (a JVM String cannot hold one). Fail closed: −2, message copied out, no JPX1 buffer | 6/6 PASS |\\\\n185: | invalid UTF-8 from jolt | a jolt string cannot carry it: jolt decodes 0xFF to U+FFFD, as the JVM's `new String` does, and its reader refuses `\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\uD800\\\\\\\"` | observed, not reachable through `cst` |\\\\n186: | output over 64 MiB (one 70 MiB string token) | −4, no buffer; `cst` throws, so no packet; the same worker parses again | PASS (also in the ASan harness) |\\\\n187: | invalid arguments: NULL source with a length, NULL out cell, NULL worker, unrecognized worker (zeroed memory) | −1, no buffer | 4/4 PASS |\\\\n188: \\\\n189: **BOM defect, found by C and fixed.**\\\\n190: - **Cause:** `ANTLRInputStream::load` strips a leading UTF-8 BOM (`runtime/src/ANTLRInputStream.cpp:43-48`); the JVM's `CharStreams.fromString` keeps it.\\\\n191: - **Effect:** jolt parsed `\\\\\\\"﻿x = 1\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"` as **ok**, with every index one code point off the source. `validate!` refused it (`token-text-not-source`), and the JVM answers a syntax error. That is a wrong *successful* packet, which C forbids.\\\\n192: - **Fix:** the wrapper's `SourceStream` (a subclass of `ANTLRInputStream`) decodes with the runtime's own `antlrcpp::Utf8::strictDecode`, throwing the same \\\\\\\"illegal byte sequence\\\\\\\" on invalid input, and keeps U+FEFF. It is used by both entries.\\\\n193: - **After the fix:** strictly equal to the JVM, and the 51 E1 entries are unchanged.\\\\n194: \\\\n195: **Also hardened:** the 64 MiB bound is now enforced **while the buffer grows** (an `Oversize` marker is thrown and answers −4). Before, the whole answer was built and only then compared with the bound.\\\\n196: \\\\n197: **Not covered:** there is no input-size bound. Only the output is bounded; the input is limited by memory and by the B recursion limits (J5).\\\\n198: \\\\n199: ## D. Leaks and lifetime\\\\n200: \\\\n201: **Cycles from jolt** (`src/dev/jolt_j0c_cycles.clj`; heap = `jolt_parser_heap_in_use`, malloc's bytes in use for the whole process; a full jolt collection before each sample):\\\\n202: \\\\n203: | run | start | end | growth | shape |\\\\n204: |---|---|---|---|---|\\\\n205: | 20,000 `cst/parse-unit` cycles over the 51 sources (+ 800 worker make/close) | 1,304,240 | 1,416,912 | +112,672 B | +103 KB in the first 1,000, then flat within 1.39–1.42 MB through 20,000 |\\\\n206: | 20,000 bare `jolt_parser_export`/`jolt_parser_free` cycles | 1,416,944 | 1,531,392 | +114,448 B | rises to ~1.53 MB by cycle 3,000, then flat |\\\\n207: \\\\n208: The 5,000-cycle run (`r4-cycles.log`) agrees.\\\\n209: \\\\n210: **AddressSanitizer + UBSan** (`bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_sanitize.clj 50`):\\\\n211: - **Builds:** the pinned runtime (the archive is checked against its SHA-256) is rebuilt with `-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all`. The standalone harness `native/jolt-parser/sanitize/harness.cpp` is linked with the wrapper and the generated parser; it is never loaded into jolt.\\\\n212: - **Each of the 50 cycles:**\\\\n213:   - worker new;\\\\n214:   - exports: ok, syntax error, lexer error, BOM, NUL, invalid UTF-8 (−2), empty;\\\\n215:   - round-1 parse: ok and syntax error;\\\\n216:   - the three fault modes on both entries;\\\\n217:   - every stats/thread/heap seam;\\\\n218:   - worker free.\\\\n219: - **Once:** the invalid arguments, the 70 MiB oversize answer (−4), then a successful re-parse, and `worker_free(NULL)`.\\\\n220: - **Result: exit 0, `HARNESS ok`, no ASan or UBSan report.**\\\\n221: \\\\n222: **Leaks:**\\\\n223: - macOS Apple clang has no LeakSanitizer, so the unsanitized build of the same harness ran under `leaks --atExit` (50 cycles): **\\\\\\\"0 leaks for 0 total leaked bytes\\\\\\\"**, exit 0.\\\\n224: - On Linux the driver sets `detect_leaks=1` for the ASan run (unrun here).\\\\n225: \\\\n226: **Not done:** TSan (the busy guard and the 4-thread run were exercised under ASan's process only, not a race detector).\\\\n227: \\\\n228: ## E. Native hardening\\\\n229: \\\\n230: - **Exported-symbols list:**\\\\n231:   - `native/jolt-parser/exports.darwin` (`_jolt_parser_*`, via `-exported_symbols_list`) and `exports.linux` (a version script, `jolt_parser_*` global, the rest local), selected in CMake.\\\\n232:   - Test build: **12 exports**. Release build: **7**: `export`, `export_layout_version`, `free`, `layout_version`, `parse`, `worker_free`, `worker_new`. Before, there were 4 entries plus about 1,556 `antlr4::` symbols (`r4-nm.log`).\\\\n233:   - **The Linux version script is unbuilt and untested.**\\\\n234: - **Test seams behind a CMake option:**\\\\n235:   - `option(JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS … OFF)` gates `jolt_parser_inject_fault`, the fault branches themselves (`maybe_fault`, `fail_handler_malloc`), and the new stats/thread/heap seams.\\\\n236:   - `bb build:jolt-native` now builds both variants: `jolt-parser/` (seams ON, what the probes and harnesses load) and `jolt-parser-release/` (CMake default, OFF). It records both, with their exported symbol lists, in `build-record.edn`.\\\\n237:   - The round-1 probe and the parity harness look the seam up (`ffi/find-symbol`). They print `SKIP … built without JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS` when it is absent; the probe shows this against the release build through `JOLT_PARSER_PROBE_LIB`.\\\\n238: - **Nonallocating failure paths are kept:** `message()` is unchanged apart from `fail_handler_malloc()`, and `Oversize` and the busy refusal allocate nothing.\\\\n239: - **Export layout version entry** (cmd G2/H3): new `jolt_parser_export_layout_version()` answers 2. `cst.jolt` checks it at load and refuses a library that writes another layout. The buffer still carries its version, and the decoder still checks that too.\\\\n240: - **dylibs:** test `2913f3ed9815f68041ca99fd9324eb2d0043bf761396640d0bb043c437d5413e`, release `9361c17641c57139dfab61d59b0e80ef9c7071010a7282f79ee362b3c973c9fd`. Both depend only on `libc++` and `libSystem`.\\\\n241: - **ABI change:** `jolt_parser_export` now takes the worker first. The JPX1 layout itself is unchanged (version 2).\\\\n242: \\\\n243: ## Commands (in the worktree) and exit codes\\\\n244: \\\\n245: Long runs: the tool moves any foreground command past 600 s to the background and stops it 10 minutes later. My first full stack run (part 1: parens, list, call), a `unary not` batch and two sanitize runs were stopped that way. The final full stack run and the 50-cycle sanitize run were then launched with `run_in_background` and a long timeout, and I waited for their completion notifications. No other command ran in the background.\\\\n246: \\\\n247: | command | exit | log |\\\\n248: |---|---|---|\\\\n249: | `bb build:jolt-native` (first: macro clash, Mach `TRUE/FALSE` vs generated token names, so the includes were moved after the generated headers; later builds after the BOM fix) | 1, then 0, 0 | `r4-build.log` |\\\\n250: | `jolt … src/dev/jolt_j0c_workers.clj` (the first runs failed the busy check on a test race; the test was fixed) | 1, 1, then 0 | `r4-workers-final.log` |\\\\n251: | `bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_stack.clj` (final, all shapes, with the 20 s per-parse cap added after unary took about 104 s at 1,024 and a run had to be stopped) | 0 | `r4-stack.log`, `build/j0c/r4/stack-budget.edn` |\\\\n252: | `jolt … src/dev/jolt_j0c_gc.clj` | 0 | `r4-gc.log` |\\\\n253: | `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle input`; `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle` (round-2 oracle regenerated, SHA-256 unchanged) | 0, 0 | `r4-input-oracle.log`, `r4-oracle-rerun.log` |\\\\n254: | parity harness (`jolt -Srepro -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj`). Intermediate runs: an NPE in the E1 checker on the BOM entry (fixed: nil-safe); `(ffi/null)` misuse (fixed). Final: 132 PASS | 1, 1, then 0 | `r4-parity-final.log` |\\\\n255: | `jolt … src/dev/jolt_j0c_cycles.clj 5000` and `… 20000` | 0, 0 | `r4-cycles.log`, `r4-cycles-20000.log` |\\\\n256: | `bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_sanitize.clj 50` (earlier: a missing `support/Utf8.h` include, exit 1; a 200-cycle run with a 200,000-statement oversize case was stopped as too slow under ASan, and the case was replaced by one large string token) | 0 | `r4-sanitize.log` |\\\\n257: | `jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` (test build; then with `JOLT_PARSER_PROBE_LIB=…/jolt-parser-release/…`) | 0 (11 PASS), 0 (7 PASS + SKIP) | `r4-probe-r1.log`, `r4-probe-r1-release.log` |\\\\n258: | `bb smoke:jolt` | 0 | `r4-smoke.log` |\\\\n259: | `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on every changed Clojure/EDN file | 0 | n/a |\\\\n260: | `clojure -M:kondo --lint` on the dev scripts | known classes only: `defcfn` names unresolved, `cst/*` resolved against the JVM `cst.clj`, a stale `validate!` arity, and the pre-existing `run!` warning in `jolt_native.clj` | n/a |\\\\n261: \\\\n262: ## Files\\\\n263: \\\\n264: **Modified:**\\\\n265: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp`: workers, the −5 busy guard, `SourceStream` (BOM), the bound enforced while growing, `TEST_SEAMS` gating, the stats/thread/heap seams, the export layout version entry.\\\\n266: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/CMakeLists.txt`: the `JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS` option, the exported-symbols list / version script.\\\\n267: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt`: native worker handle, `close-worker`, worker-taking `export-records`, the layout version check at load.\\\\n268: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj`: builds the test and release variants, records their exported symbols.\\\\n269: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj`: the fault checks skip without the seam; `JOLT_PARSER_PROBE_LIB`.\\\\n270: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj`: workers closed, the input-policy section, a nil-safe E1 checker, the fault checks skip without the seam.\\\\n271: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj`: the `input` mode.\\\\n272: \\\\n273: **Added:**\\\\n274: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/exports.darwin`\\\\n275: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/exports.linux`\\\\n276: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/sanitize/CMakeLists.txt`\\\\n277: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/sanitize/harness.cpp`\\\\n278: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_workers.clj`\\\\n279: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_stack.clj`\\\\n280: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_stack_child.clj`\\\\n281: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_gc.clj`\\\\n282: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_cycles.clj`\\\\n283: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_sanitize.clj`\\\\n284: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_input_corpus.edn`\\\\n285: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_input_oracle.edn` (written by the JVM)\\\\n286: \\\\n287: **Untouched:** the round-2/3 corpus and oracle, the manifest and its recorded digests, `docs/design`, the shared exporter and packet code, and dao.stream, dao.data, dao.jing and yin.vm.\\\\n288: \\\\n289: Report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792500314000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r4.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\\\"}]}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"e55fc800-60e8-4261-9cb0-055a7dfc3af3\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Okay, now let me adversarially read through the C++ — worker ownership, busy guard, SourceStream BOM fix, Oversize, seam gating, non-allocating paths. The file is currently probably around 700 lines. Going to read it.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Now the wrapper source, the key adversarial target.\\\\n\\\\n\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_ac06bcfc4a574f019818ed5f\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_file\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"file_path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp\\\"}}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"model\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792508093589,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"59fe5c36-19ff-47e7-b381-e0c9d2e66b4c\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_ac06bcfc4a574f019818ed5f\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Read 1/1 file, 694 lines\\\\n1: // jolt-parser: the extern \\\\\\\"C\\\\\\\" boundary between jolt and the ANTLR4 C++\\\\n2: // Python3 parser (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.3).\\\\n3: //\\\\n4: // J0c round 1 probe surface. Bytes in, bytes out: the caller passes UTF-8\\\\n5: // source by pointer and length and receives one private marshalling buffer,\\\\n6: // which it copies and returns through jolt_parser_free. No C++ class, layout\\\\n7: // or exception crosses this boundary; every entry catches std::exception and\\\\n8: // (...) and answers an explicit status. This is process-local marshalling (like\\\\n9: // a sockaddr), not a value codec: it never enters a stream. Packets are built\\\\n10: // by the shared jolt/Clojure exporter from these records, never here.\\\\n11: //\\\\n12: // Buffer layout, all integers little-endian:\\\\n13: //\\\\n14: //   magic   \\\\\\\"JPT1\\\\\\\"\\\\n15: //   u32     layout version (1)\\\\n16: //   u32     status (0 ok, 1 syntax errors)\\\\n17: //   u32     rule-name count R,     then R  x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\\\\n18: //   u32     symbolic-name count S, then S  x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\\\\n19: //   u32     node count N, then N preorder records:\\\\n20: //             u8  kind (0 rule, 1 token)\\\\n21: //             rule:  u32 rule index, u32 child count\\\\n22: //             token: i32 token type, u32 start, u32 stop (code-point indexes,\\\\n23: //                    0xFFFFFFFF = none), u32 text len, UTF-8 text\\\\n24: //   u32     error count E, then E x (u32 line, u32 column, u32 len, UTF-8 msg)\\\\n25: //\\\\n26: // Status codes returned by jolt_parser_parse:\\\\n27: //    0  parsed, buffer written (status field says whether errors occurred)\\\\n28: //   -1  invalid arguments\\\\n29: //   -2  C++ exception (std::exception); *out holds its message, UTF-8\\\\n30: //   -3  unknown exception\\\\n31: // On -2/-3 the message buffer is built without anything that can throw\\\\n32: // (strlen/malloc/memcpy only); if even that malloc fails, *out stays NULL and\\\\n33: // only the status reports the failure. No handler can throw.\\\\n34: //\\\\n35: //   -4  output would exceed the bound (64 MiB), checked while the buffer\\\\n36: //       grows, so an oversize answer is never built in full\\\\n37: //   -5  the worker is in use by another call (a worker serves one thread at\\\\n38: //       a time; this call did nothing)\\\\n39: //\\\\n40: // jolt_parser_parse (J0c round 1) recognizes with the generated static\\\\n41: // caches. jolt_parser_export takes a worker (§7.4, round 4): an opaque handle\\\\n42: // from jolt_parser_worker_new that owns its lexer and parser DFA vectors and\\\\n43: // PredictionContextCaches; each call installs fresh Lexer/ParserATNSimulators\\\\n44: // over them in place of the generated defaults (which point at the static\\\\n45: // caches) before recognition. The ATNs are the generated immutable tables and\\\\n46: // stay shared. jolt_parser_worker_free disposes the handle.\\\\n47: //\\\\n48: // TEST SEAMS, compiled only with -DJOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS=ON (CMake option,\\\\n49: // default OFF): jolt_parser_inject_fault makes the next parses fail\\\\n50: // deterministically so the containment paths can be shown without\\\\n51: // exhausting memory; jolt_parser_static_cache_stats and\\\\n52: // jolt_parser_worker_cache_stats count cache contents;\\\\n53: // jolt_parser_thread_info reports the calling thread's stack;\\\\n54: // jolt_parser_heap_in_use reports malloc's bytes in use.\\\\n55: //\\\\n56: // jolt_parser_export answers the records the jolt yang.antlr.cst adapter\\\\n57: // hands to the shared exporter (§7.3). Indexes are i32, -1 for ANTLR's\\\\n58: // \\\\\\\"none\\\\\\\" (INVALID_INDEX, a stop before a zero-width start), as the JVM\\\\n59: // reports them. Its layout (version from jolt_parser_export_layout_version):\\\\n60: //\\\\n61: //   magic   \\\\\\\"JPX1\\\\\\\"\\\\n62: //   u32     layout version (2; 2 added the offending token type)\\\\n63: //   u32     status (0 ok, 1 syntax errors)\\\\n64: //   u32     rule-name count R,     then R x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\\\\n65: //   u32     symbolic-name count S, then S x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes; \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" none)\\\\n66: //   u32     literal-name count S,  then S x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes; \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" none)\\\\n67: //   u32     node count N, then N preorder records:\\\\n68: //             u8  kind (0 rule, 1 token)\\\\n69: //             rule:  u32 rule index, u32 child count,\\\\n70: //                    u8 has-start, i32 context start (start token's start)\\\\n71: //             token: i32 type, i32 start, i32 stop, i32 token index,\\\\n72: //                    u32 text len, UTF-8 text\\\\n73: //   u32     error count E, then E x\\\\n74: //             u32 line, u32 column, u32 len, UTF-8 message,\\\\n75: //             u8 span kind (0 none, 1 offending token, 2 lexer),\\\\n76: //             i32 a, i32 b (token start/stop, or lexer token-start char\\\\n77: //             index / input index when the error was reported),\\\\n78: //             i32 offending token type (-2 when there is no token)\\\\n79: //   first-failure evidence (the first error reported, lexer or parser):\\\\n80: //             u8 kind (0 none, 1 lexical, 2 token), u8 no-viable-alt,\\\\n81: //             i32 offending token index, u32 tokens buffered at the report\\\\n82: //   u32     buffered token count T after the parse, then T x\\\\n83: //             (i32 type, i32 token index, i32 start, i32 stop)\\\\n84: \\\\n85: #include <atomic>\\\\n86: #include <cstdint>\\\\n87: #include <cstdlib>\\\\n88: #include <cstring>\\\\n89: #include <limits>\\\\n90: #include <string>\\\\n91: #include <string_view>\\\\n92: #include <vector>\\\\n93: \\\\n94: #include \\\\\\\"antlr4-runtime.h\\\\\\\"\\\\n95: #include \\\\\\\"support/Utf8.h\\\\\\\"\\\\n96: #include \\\\\\\"Python3Lexer.h\\\\\\\"\\\\n97: #include \\\\\\\"Python3Parser.h\\\\\\\"\\\\n98: \\\\n99: // After the generated headers: Mach's headers define TRUE/FALSE, which are\\\\n100: // also generated token names.\\\\n101: #include <pthread.h>\\\\n102: #if defined(__APPLE__)\\\\n103: #include <malloc/malloc.h>\\\\n104: #elif defined(__GLIBC__)\\\\n105: #include <malloc.h>\\\\n106: #include <sys/syscall.h>\\\\n107: #include <unistd.h>\\\\n108: #endif\\\\n109: \\\\n110: #define JOLT_EXPORT extern \\\\\\\"C\\\\\\\" __attribute__((visibility(\\\\\\\"default\\\\\\\")))\\\\n111: \\\\n112: namespace {\\\\n113: \\\\n114: constexpr size_t kMaxOutput = 64u * 1024u * 1024u;\\\\n115: \\\\n116: #ifdef JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS\\\\n117: // 0 off; 1 throw std::bad_alloc inside the parse; 2 throw a non-std\\\\n118: // exception; 3 as 1, and the failure handler's malloc fails too.\\\\n119: std::atomic<int32_t> g_fault{0};\\\\n120: \\\\n121: void maybe_fault() {\\\\n122:   switch (g_fault.load()) {\\\\n123:     case 1:\\\\n124:     case 3: throw std::bad_alloc();\\\\n125:     case 2: throw 42;\\\\n126:     default: break;\\\\n127:   }\\\\n128: }\\\\n129: \\\\n130: bool fail_handler_malloc() { return g_fault.load() == 3; }\\\\n131: #else\\\\n132: void maybe_fault() {}\\\\n133: bool fail_handler_malloc() { return false; }\\\\n134: #endif\\\\n135: \\\\n136: // Thrown (and caught by the entries) when the buffer would pass kMaxOutput.\\\\n137: struct Oversize {};\\\\n138: \\\\n139: struct Out {\\\\n140:   std::vector<uint8_t> buf;\\\\n141:   void room(size_t n) {\\\\n142:     if (n > kMaxOutput - buf.size()) throw Oversize{};\\\\n143:   }\\\\n144:   void u8(uint8_t v) {\\\\n145:     room(1);\\\\n146:     buf.push_back(v);\\\\n147:   }\\\\n148:   void u32(uint32_t v) {\\\\n149:     room(4);\\\\n150:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) buf.push_back(static_cast<uint8_t>(v >> (8 * i)));\\\\n151:   }\\\\n152:   void str(std::string_view s) {\\\\n153:     u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(s.size()));\\\\n154:     room(s.size());\\\\n155:     buf.insert(buf.end(), s.begin(), s.end());\\\\n156:   }\\\\n157: };\\\\n158: \\\\n159: uint32_t idx(size_t i) {\\\\n160:   return i > std::numeric_limits<uint32_t>::max()\\\\n161:              ? std::numeric_limits<uint32_t>::max()\\\\n162:              : static_cast<uint32_t>(i);\\\\n163: }\\\\n164: \\\\n165: struct Error {\\\\n166:   uint32_t line, column;\\\\n167:   std::string msg;\\\\n168: };\\\\n169: \\\\n170: class Collect : public antlr4::BaseErrorListener {\\\\n171:  public:\\\\n172:   std::vector<Error> errors;\\\\n173:   void syntaxError(antlr4::Recognizer*, antlr4::Token*, size_t line,\\\\n174:                    size_t column, const std::string& msg,\\\\n175:                    std::exception_ptr) override {\\\\n176:     errors.push_back({idx(line), idx(column), msg});\\\\n177:   }\\\\n178: };\\\\n179: \\\\n180: void walk(antlr4::tree::ParseTree* t, Out& out, uint32_t& count) {\\\\n181:   ++count;\\\\n182:   if (auto* term = dynamic_cast<antlr4::tree::TerminalNode*>(t)) {\\\\n183:     antlr4::Token* tok = term->getSymbol();\\\\n184:     out.u8(1);\\\\n185:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(tok->getType())));\\\\n186:     out.u32(idx(tok->getStartIndex()));\\\\n187:     out.u32(idx(tok->getStopIndex()));\\\\n188:     out.str(tok->getText());\\\\n189:     return;\\\\n190:   }\\\\n191:   auto* ctx = dynamic_cast<antlr4::ParserRuleContext*>(t);\\\\n192:   out.u8(0);\\\\n193:   out.u32(idx(ctx->getRuleIndex()));\\\\n194:   out.u32(idx(ctx->children.size()));\\\\n195:   for (auto* c : ctx->children) walk(c, out, count);\\\\n196: }\\\\n197: \\\\n198: int32_t hand_off(const std::vector<uint8_t>& bytes, uint8_t** out,\\\\n199:                  size_t* out_len) {\\\\n200:   if (bytes.size() > kMaxOutput) return -4;\\\\n201:   auto* p = static_cast<uint8_t*>(std::malloc(bytes.empty() ? 1 : bytes.size()));\\\\n202:   if (!p) return -3;\\\\n203:   if (!bytes.empty()) std::memcpy(p, bytes.data(), bytes.size());\\\\n204:   *out = p;\\\\n205:   *out_len = bytes.size();\\\\n206:   return 0;\\\\n207: }\\\\n208: \\\\n209: // The failure answer: `status`, plus the message copied into a malloc'd\\\\n210: // buffer when that is possible. Nonallocating apart from that one malloc,\\\\n211: // whose failure leaves *out NULL. Never throws.\\\\n212: int32_t message(const char* what, int32_t status, uint8_t** out,\\\\n213:                 size_t* out_len) noexcept {\\\\n214:   *out = nullptr;\\\\n215:   *out_len = 0;\\\\n216:   const char* m = what ? what : \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\";\\\\n217:   size_t n = std::strlen(m);\\\\n218:   void* p = fail_handler_malloc() ? nullptr : std::malloc(n == 0 ? 1 : n);\\\\n219:   if (p) {\\\\n220:     std::memcpy(p, m, n);\\\\n221:     *out = static_cast<uint8_t*>(p);\\\\n222:     *out_len = n;\\\\n223:   }\\\\n224:   return status;\\\\n225: }\\\\n226: \\\\n227: // --- jolt_parser_export ------------------------------------------------------\\\\n228: \\\\n229: int32_t sidx(size_t i) {\\\\n230:   if (i == std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max()) return -1;  // INVALID_INDEX\\\\n231:   return i > static_cast<size_t>(std::numeric_limits<int32_t>::max())\\\\n232:              ? std::numeric_limits<int32_t>::max()\\\\n233:              : static_cast<int32_t>(i);\\\\n234: }\\\\n235: \\\\n236: struct ExportError {\\\\n237:   uint32_t line, column;\\\\n238:   std::string msg;\\\\n239:   uint8_t span_kind;\\\\n240:   int32_t a, b;\\\\n241:   int32_t type;\\\\n242: };\\\\n243: \\\\n244: class ExportCollect : public antlr4::BaseErrorListener {\\\\n245:  public:\\\\n246:   explicit ExportCollect(antlr4::CommonTokenStream* tokens) : tokens_(tokens) {}\\\\n247:   std::vector<ExportError> errors;\\\\n248:   uint8_t failure_kind = 0;  // 0 none, 1 lexical, 2 token\\\\n249:   uint8_t no_viable = 0;\\\\n250:   int32_t failure_index = -1;\\\\n251:   uint32_t buffered = 0;\\\\n252: \\\\n253:   void syntaxError(antlr4::Recognizer* recognizer, antlr4::Token* offending,\\\\n254:                    size_t line, size_t column, const std::string& msg,\\\\n255:                    std::exception_ptr e) override {\\\\n256:     ExportError err{idx(line), idx(column), msg, 0, -1, -1, -2};\\\\n257:     auto* lexer = dynamic_cast<antlr4::Lexer*>(recognizer);\\\\n258:     if (offending) {\\\\n259:       err.span_kind = 1;\\\\n260:       err.a = sidx(offending->getStartIndex());\\\\n261:       err.b = sidx(offending->getStopIndex());\\\\n262:       err.type = static_cast<int32_t>(offending->getType());\\\\n263:     } else if (lexer) {\\\\n264:       err.span_kind = 2;\\\\n265:       err.a = sidx(lexer->tokenStartCharIndex);\\\\n266:       err.b = sidx(lexer->getInputStream()->index());\\\\n267:     }\\\\n268:     if (failure_kind == 0) {\\\\n269:       if (!offending) {\\\\n270:         failure_kind = 1;\\\\n271:       } else {\\\\n272:         failure_kind = 2;\\\\n273:         failure_index = sidx(offending->getTokenIndex());\\\\n274:         if (e) {\\\\n275:           try {\\\\n276:             std::rethrow_exception(e);\\\\n277:           } catch (const antlr4::NoViableAltException&) {\\\\n278:             no_viable = 1;\\\\n279:           } catch (...) {\\\\n280:           }\\\\n281:         }\\\\n282:       }\\\\n283:       buffered = idx(tokens_->getTokens().size());\\\\n284:     }\\\\n285:     errors.push_back(std::move(err));\\\\n286:   }\\\\n287: \\\\n288:  private:\\\\n289:   antlr4::CommonTokenStream* tokens_;\\\\n290: };\\\\n291: \\\\n292: void walk_export(antlr4::tree::ParseTree* t, Out& out, uint32_t& count) {\\\\n293:   ++count;\\\\n294:   if (auto* term = dynamic_cast<antlr4::tree::TerminalNode*>(t)) {\\\\n295:     antlr4::Token* tok = term->getSymbol();\\\\n296:     out.u8(1);\\\\n297:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(tok->getType())));\\\\n298:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(tok->getStartIndex())));\\\\n299:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(tok->getStopIndex())));\\\\n300:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(tok->getTokenIndex())));\\\\n301:     out.str(tok->getText());\\\\n302:     return;\\\\n303:   }\\\\n304:   auto* ctx = dynamic_cast<antlr4::ParserRuleContext*>(t);\\\\n305:   out.u8(0);\\\\n306:   out.u32(idx(ctx->getRuleIndex()));\\\\n307:   out.u32(idx(ctx->children.size()));\\\\n308:   out.u8(ctx->start ? 1 : 0);\\\\n309:   out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(ctx->start ? sidx(ctx->start->getStartIndex()) : -1));\\\\n310:   for (auto* c : ctx->children) walk_export(c, out, count);\\\\n311: }\\\\n312: \\\\n313: // The source as code points, exactly: a strict UTF-8 decode that throws on an\\\\n314: // illegal sequence (as ANTLRInputStream's own does) but keeps a leading\\\\n315: // U+FEFF. ANTLRInputStream::load strips a UTF-8 BOM\\\\n316: // (runtime/src/ANTLRInputStream.cpp:43-48); the JVM's CharStreams.fromString\\\\n317: // does not, and a stripped BOM shifts every index against the source the\\\\n318: // exporter maps spans onto (J0c round 4 input policy).\\\\n319: class SourceStream : public antlr4::ANTLRInputStream {\\\\n320:  public:\\\\n321:   explicit SourceStream(std::string_view utf8) {\\\\n322:     auto cps = antlrcpp::Utf8::strictDecode(utf8);\\\\n323:     if (!cps.has_value())\\\\n324:       throw antlr4::IllegalArgumentException(\\\\n325:           \\\\\\\"UTF-8 string contains an illegal byte sequence\\\\\\\");\\\\n326:     _data = std::move(cps).value();\\\\n327:     p = 0;\\\\n328:   }\\\\n329: };\\\\n330: \\\\n331: // --- workers (§7.4) -----------------------------------------------------------\\\\n332: \\\\n333: constexpr uint64_t kWorkerTag = 0x4a504152534552ull;  // \\\\\\\"JPARSER\\\\\\\"\\\\n334: \\\\n335: std::vector<antlr4::dfa::DFA> fresh_dfa(const antlr4::atn::ATN& atn) {\\\\n336:   std::vector<antlr4::dfa::DFA> dfa;\\\\n337:   const size_t n = atn.getNumberOfDecisions();\\\\n338:   dfa.reserve(n);\\\\n339:   for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) dfa.emplace_back(atn.getDecisionState(i), i);\\\\n340:   return dfa;\\\\n341: }\\\\n342: \\\\n343: // The generated, immutable ATNs (static data initialized once by the\\\\n344: // generated constructors).\\\\n345: struct Atns {\\\\n346:   const antlr4::atn::ATN* lexer;\\\\n347:   const antlr4::atn::ATN* parser;\\\\n348: };\\\\n349: \\\\n350: Atns generated_atns() {\\\\n351:   antlr4::ANTLRInputStream input(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");\\\\n352:   Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\\\\n353:   antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\\\\n354:   Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\\\\n355:   return {&lexer.getATN(), &parser.getATN()};\\\\n356: }\\\\n357: \\\\n358: struct Worker {\\\\n359:   uint64_t tag = kWorkerTag;\\\\n360:   const antlr4::atn::ATN* lexer_atn;\\\\n361:   const antlr4::atn::ATN* parser_atn;\\\\n362:   std::vector<antlr4::dfa::DFA> lexer_dfa;\\\\n363:   std::vector<antlr4::dfa::DFA> parser_dfa;\\\\n364:   antlr4::atn::PredictionContextCache lexer_ctx;\\\\n365:   antlr4::atn::PredictionContextCache parser_ctx;\\\\n366:   std::atomic<bool> busy{false};\\\\n367: \\\\n368:   explicit Worker(Atns atns)\\\\n369:       : lexer_atn(atns.lexer),\\\\n370:         parser_atn(atns.parser),\\\\n371:         lexer_dfa(fresh_dfa(*atns.lexer)),\\\\n372:         parser_dfa(fresh_dfa(*atns.parser)) {}\\\\n373: };\\\\n374: \\\\n375: Worker* as_worker(void* w) {\\\\n376:   auto* worker = static_cast<Worker*>(w);\\\\n377:   return worker && worker->tag == kWorkerTag ? worker : nullptr;\\\\n378: }\\\\n379: \\\\n380: // Holds a worker for one call; a second concurrent call is refused.\\\\n381: struct Hold {\\\\n382:   Worker* w;\\\\n383:   bool held;\\\\n384:   explicit Hold(Worker* worker) : w(worker) {\\\\n385:     bool expected = false;\\\\n386:     held = w->busy.compare_exchange_strong(expected, true);\\\\n387:   }\\\\n388:   ~Hold() {\\\\n389:     if (held) w->busy.store(false);\\\\n390:   }\\\\n391: };\\\\n392: \\\\n393: }  // namespace\\\\n394: \\\\n395: // A new worker owning fresh DFA vectors and PredictionContextCaches. NULL when\\\\n396: // it cannot be made (nothing throws across the boundary).\\\\n397: JOLT_EXPORT void* jolt_parser_worker_new(void) {\\\\n398:   try {\\\\n399:     return new Worker(generated_atns());\\\\n400:   } catch (...) {\\\\n401:     return nullptr;\\\\n402:   }\\\\n403: }\\\\n404: \\\\n405: // Dispose a worker and its caches. NULL is accepted. The caller must not use\\\\n406: // the handle again, nor free it while a call on it runs.\\\\n407: JOLT_EXPORT void jolt_parser_worker_free(void* w) {\\\\n408:   if (Worker* worker = as_worker(w)) {\\\\n409:     worker->tag = 0;\\\\n410:     delete worker;\\\\n411:   }\\\\n412: }\\\\n413: \\\\n414: // Parse UTF-8 `src[0, len)` from `file_input` with `worker`'s caches and\\\\n415: // answer the exporter's records (layout \\\\\\\"JPX1\\\\\\\" above). Statuses as\\\\n416: // jolt_parser_parse, plus -5; -1 also for a NULL or unrecognized worker.\\\\n417: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_export(void* worker, const uint8_t* src,\\\\n418:                                        size_t len, uint8_t** out,\\\\n419:                                        size_t* out_len) {\\\\n420:   if (!out || !out_len || (!src && len != 0)) return -1;\\\\n421:   *out = nullptr;\\\\n422:   *out_len = 0;\\\\n423:   Worker* w = as_worker(worker);\\\\n424:   if (!w) return -1;\\\\n425:   Hold hold(w);\\\\n426:   if (!hold.held) return -5;\\\\n427:   try {\\\\n428:     maybe_fault();\\\\n429:     SourceStream input(\\\\n430:         std::string_view(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(src), len));\\\\n431:     Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\\\\n432:     // setInterpreter deletes the generated default simulator, the one over\\\\n433:     // the static caches; the generated destructors delete these at scope exit\\\\n434:     lexer.setInterpreter(new antlr4::atn::LexerATNSimulator(\\\\n435:         &lexer, *w->lexer_atn, w->lexer_dfa, w->lexer_ctx));\\\\n436:     antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\\\\n437:     Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\\\\n438:     parser.setInterpreter(new antlr4::atn::ParserATNSimulator(\\\\n439:         &parser, *w->parser_atn, w->parser_dfa, w->parser_ctx));\\\\n440:     ExportCollect errors(&tokens);\\\\n441:     lexer.removeErrorListeners();\\\\n442:     parser.removeErrorListeners();\\\\n443:     lexer.addErrorListener(&errors);\\\\n444:     parser.addErrorListener(&errors);\\\\n445: \\\\n446:     antlr4::tree::ParseTree* tree = parser.file_input();\\\\n447: \\\\n448:     Out o;\\\\n449:     o.buf.insert(o.buf.end(), {'J', 'P', 'X', '1'});\\\\n450:     o.u32(2);\\\\n451:     o.u32(errors.errors.empty() ? 0 : 1);\\\\n452:     const auto& rules = parser.getRuleNames();\\\\n453:     o.u32(idx(rules.size()));\\\\n454:     for (const auto& r : rules) o.str(r);\\\\n455:     const auto& vocab = parser.getVocabulary();\\\\n456:     size_t max_type = vocab.getMaxTokenType();\\\\n457:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\\\\n458:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getSymbolicName(i));\\\\n459:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\\\\n460:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getLiteralName(i));\\\\n461:     size_t count_at = o.buf.size();\\\\n462:     o.u32(0);\\\\n463:     uint32_t count = 0;\\\\n464:     walk_export(tree, o, count);\\\\n465:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\\\\n466:       o.buf[count_at + i] = static_cast<uint8_t>(count >> (8 * i));\\\\n467:     o.u32(idx(errors.errors.size()));\\\\n468:     for (const auto& e : errors.errors) {\\\\n469:       o.u32(e.line);\\\\n470:       o.u32(e.column);\\\\n471:       o.str(e.msg);\\\\n472:       o.u8(e.span_kind);\\\\n473:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(e.a));\\\\n474:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(e.b));\\\\n475:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(e.type));\\\\n476:     }\\\\n477:     o.u8(errors.failure_kind);\\\\n478:     o.u8(errors.no_viable);\\\\n479:     o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(errors.failure_index));\\\\n480:     o.u32(errors.buffered);\\\\n481:     const auto buffered = tokens.getTokens();\\\\n482:     o.u32(idx(buffered.size()));\\\\n483:     for (auto* t : buffered) {\\\\n484:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(t->getType())));\\\\n485:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(t->getTokenIndex())));\\\\n486:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(t->getStartIndex())));\\\\n487:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(t->getStopIndex())));\\\\n488:     }\\\\n489:     return hand_off(o.buf, out, out_len);\\\\n490:   } catch (const Oversize&) {\\\\n491:     return -4;\\\\n492:   } catch (const std::exception& e) {\\\\n493:     return message(e.what(), -2, out, out_len);\\\\n494:   } catch (...) {\\\\n495:     return message(\\\\\\\"unknown exception\\\\\\\", -3, out, out_len);\\\\n496:   }\\\\n497: }\\\\n498: \\\\n499: // Parse UTF-8 `src[0, len)` from the `file_input` rule, with the generated\\\\n500: // static caches (the round-1 probe surface).\\\\n501: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_parse(const uint8_t* src, size_t len,\\\\n502:                                       uint8_t** out, size_t* out_len) {\\\\n503:   if (!out || !out_len || (!src && len != 0)) return -1;\\\\n504:   *out = nullptr;\\\\n505:   *out_len = 0;\\\\n506:   try {\\\\n507:     maybe_fault();\\\\n508:     SourceStream input(\\\\n509:         std::string_view(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(src), len));\\\\n510:     Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\\\\n511:     antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\\\\n512:     Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\\\\n513:     Collect errors;\\\\n514:     lexer.removeErrorListeners();\\\\n515:     parser.removeErrorListeners();\\\\n516:     lexer.addErrorListener(&errors);\\\\n517:     parser.addErrorListener(&errors);\\\\n518: \\\\n519:     antlr4::tree::ParseTree* tree = parser.file_input();\\\\n520: \\\\n521:     Out o;\\\\n522:     o.buf.insert(o.buf.end(), {'J', 'P', 'T', '1'});\\\\n523:     o.u32(1);\\\\n524:     o.u32(errors.errors.empty() ? 0 : 1);\\\\n525:     const auto& rules = parser.getRuleNames();\\\\n526:     o.u32(idx(rules.size()));\\\\n527:     for (const auto& r : rules) o.str(r);\\\\n528:     const auto& vocab = parser.getVocabulary();\\\\n529:     size_t max_type = vocab.getMaxTokenType();\\\\n530:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\\\\n531:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getSymbolicName(i));\\\\n532:     size_t count_at = o.buf.size();\\\\n533:     o.u32(0);\\\\n534:     uint32_t count = 0;\\\\n535:     walk(tree, o, count);\\\\n536:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\\\\n537:       o.buf[count_at + i] = static_cast<uint8_t>(count >> (8 * i));\\\\n538:     o.u32(idx(errors.errors.size()));\\\\n539:     for (const auto& e : errors.errors) {\\\\n540:       o.u32(e.line);\\\\n541:       o.u32(e.column);\\\\n542:       o.str(e.msg);\\\\n543:     }\\\\n544:     return hand_off(o.buf, out, out_len);\\\\n545:   } catch (const Oversize&) {\\\\n546:     return -4;\\\\n547:   } catch (const std::exception& e) {\\\\n548:     return message(e.what(), -2, out, out_len);\\\\n549:   } catch (...) {\\\\n550:     return message(\\\\\\\"unknown exception\\\\\\\", -3, out, out_len);\\\\n551:   }\\\\n552: }\\\\n553: \\\\n554: // Release a buffer jolt_parser_parse handed out. NULL is accepted.\\\\n555: JOLT_EXPORT void jolt_parser_free(uint8_t* p) { std::free(p); }\\\\n556: \\\\n557: // The JPT1 layout version jolt_parser_parse writes.\\\\n558: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_layout_version(void) { return 1; }\\\\n559: \\\\n560: // The JPX1 layout version jolt_parser_export writes.\\\\n561: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_export_layout_version(void) { return 2; }\\\\n562: \\\\n563: #ifdef JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS\\\\n564: \\\\n565: // TEST SEAM: set the fault mode (0 off, 1-3 as g_fault above) for later\\\\n566: // parses. Answers the previous mode.\\\\n567: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_inject_fault(int32_t mode) {\\\\n568:   return g_fault.exchange(mode);\\\\n569: }\\\\n570: \\\\n571: namespace {\\\\n572: \\\\n573: // Reading two private counters for the stats below, through the standard\\\\n574: // explicit-instantiation access idiom (test seam only; reads, never writes).\\\\n575: template <auto M, typename Tag>\\\\n576: struct Peek {\\\\n577:   friend auto peek(Tag) { return M; }\\\\n578: };\\\\n579: struct CtxData {};\\\\n580: struct NextUpdated {};\\\\n581: auto peek(CtxData);\\\\n582: auto peek(NextUpdated);\\\\n583: template struct Peek<&antlr4::atn::PredictionContextCache::_data, CtxData>;\\\\n584: template struct Peek<&antlr4::atn::ATNState::_nextTokenUpdated, NextUpdated>;\\\\n585: \\\\n586: void dfa_counts(const std::vector<antlr4::dfa::DFA>& dfas, uint64_t& states,\\\\n587:                 uint64_t& edges) {\\\\n588:   for (const auto& d : dfas) {\\\\n589:     states += d.states.size() + (d.s0 ? 1 : 0);\\\\n590:     if (d.s0) edges += d.s0->edges.size();\\\\n591:     for (const auto* st : d.states) edges += st->edges.size();\\\\n592:   }\\\\n593: }\\\\n594: \\\\n595: uint64_t ctx_size(const antlr4::atn::PredictionContextCache& c) {\\\\n596:   return (c.*peek(CtxData{})).size();\\\\n597: }\\\\n598: \\\\n599: uint64_t memo_count(const antlr4::atn::ATN& atn) {\\\\n600:   uint64_t n = 0;\\\\n601:   for (auto* st : atn.states)\\\\n602:     if (st && (st->*peek(NextUpdated{})).load()) ++n;\\\\n603:   return n;\\\\n604: }\\\\n605: \\\\n606: }  // namespace\\\\n607: \\\\n608: // TEST SEAM: the generated static caches and the shared ATNs' lazily filled\\\\n609: // next-token memo. out[0..7] = lexer DFA states, lexer DFA edges, lexer\\\\n610: // context-cache entries, parser DFA states, parser DFA edges, parser\\\\n611: // context-cache entries, lexer ATN states with a filled next-token memo,\\\\n612: // parser ATN states with one. Call with no parse running.\\\\n613: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_static_cache_stats(uint64_t* out) {\\\\n614:   if (!out) return -1;\\\\n615:   try {\\\\n616:     antlr4::ANTLRInputStream input(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");\\\\n617:     Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\\\\n618:     antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\\\\n619:     Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\\\\n620:     auto* ls = lexer.getInterpreter<antlr4::atn::LexerATNSimulator>();\\\\n621:     auto* ps = parser.getInterpreter<antlr4::atn::ParserATNSimulator>();\\\\n622:     for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) out[i] = 0;\\\\n623:     dfa_counts(ls->_decisionToDFA, out[0], out[1]);\\\\n624:     out[2] = ctx_size(ls->getSharedContextCache());\\\\n625:     dfa_counts(ps->decisionToDFA, out[3], out[4]);\\\\n626:     out[5] = ctx_size(ps->getSharedContextCache());\\\\n627:     out[6] = memo_count(lexer.getATN());\\\\n628:     out[7] = memo_count(parser.getATN());\\\\n629:     return 0;\\\\n630:   } catch (...) {\\\\n631:     return -3;\\\\n632:   }\\\\n633: }\\\\n634: \\\\n635: // TEST SEAM: a worker's own caches, out[0..5] as out[0..5] above.\\\\n636: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_worker_cache_stats(void* worker, uint64_t* out) {\\\\n637:   Worker* w = as_worker(worker);\\\\n638:   if (!w || !out) return -1;\\\\n639:   for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i) out[i] = 0;\\\\n640:   dfa_counts(w->lexer_dfa, out[0], out[1]);\\\\n641:   out[2] = ctx_size(w->lexer_ctx);\\\\n642:   dfa_counts(w->parser_dfa, out[3], out[4]);\\\\n643:   out[5] = ctx_size(w->parser_ctx);\\\\n644:   return 0;\\\\n645: }\\\\n646: \\\\n647: // TEST SEAM: the calling thread's stack. out[0..4] = stack size in bytes,\\\\n648: // lowest and highest stack address, the current stack position, 1 when it is\\\\n649: // the process's main thread.\\\\n650: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_thread_info(uint64_t* out) {\\\\n651:   if (!out) return -1;\\\\n652:   volatile char here = 0;\\\\n653:   out[3] = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(&here);\\\\n654: #if defined(__APPLE__)\\\\n655:   pthread_t self = pthread_self();\\\\n656:   uint64_t size = pthread_get_stacksize_np(self);\\\\n657:   uint64_t high = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(pthread_get_stackaddr_np(self));\\\\n658:   out[0] = size;\\\\n659:   out[1] = high - size;\\\\n660:   out[2] = high;\\\\n661:   out[4] = pthread_main_np() == 1 ? 1 : 0;\\\\n662:   return 0;\\\\n663: #elif defined(__GLIBC__)\\\\n664:   pthread_attr_t attr;\\\\n665:   if (pthread_getattr_np(pthread_self(), &attr) != 0) return -3;\\\\n666:   void* low = nullptr;\\\\n667:   size_t size = 0;\\\\n668:   pthread_attr_getstack(&attr, &low, &size);\\\\n669:   pthread_attr_destroy(&attr);\\\\n670:   out[0] = size;\\\\n671:   out[1] = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(low);\\\\n672:   out[2] = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(low) + size;\\\\n673:   out[4] = getpid() == static_cast<pid_t>(syscall(SYS_gettid)) ? 1 : 0;\\\\n674:   return 0;\\\\n675: #else\\\\n676:   return -3;\\\\n677: #endif\\\\n678: }\\\\n679: \\\\n680: // TEST SEAM: bytes malloc reports in use (all zones on macOS, glibc's\\\\n681: // mallinfo2 on Linux; 0 elsewhere).\\\\n682: JOLT_EXPORT uint64_t jolt_parser_heap_in_use(void) {\\\\n683: #if defined(__APPLE__)\\\\n684:   malloc_statistics_t st;\\\\n685:   malloc_zone_statistics(nullptr, &st);\\\\n686:   return st.size_in_use;\\\\n687: #elif defined(__GLIBC__) && (__GLIBC__ > 2 || (__GLIBC__ == 2 && __GLIBC_MINOR__ >= 33))\\\\n688:   return mallinfo2().uordblks;\\\\n689: #else\\\\n690:   return 0;\\\\n691: #endif\\\\n692: }\\\\n693: \\\\n694: #endif  // JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS\\\"}]}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"5cf0051a-6316-4d3a-9af1-cfe7402afbac\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"C++ looks robust. Adversarial observations:\\\\n\\\\n1. Worker ownership: Worker owns DFA vector + PredictionContextCache; per-call a new simulator is installed via setInterpreter. Need to verify that LexerATNSimulator/ParserATNSimulator constructors take a DFA vector reference + ctx cache — yes, standard ANTLR C++ signatures. setInterpreter deletes the previous one (Recognizer.cpp claim). At scope exit the generated destructor deletes the interpreter — in the ANTLR C++ generated lexer/parser destructors, is it `delete _interpreter;`? Lexer's destructor: generated Python3Lexer::~Python3Lexer() { delete _interpreter; }? In the actual ANTLR C++ runtime, the Lexer base class destructor doesn't delete the interpreter; does the generated code have `~Python3Lexer() override { delete getInterpreter(); }`? Hmm. Actually, generated ANTLR C++ lexer: `Python3Lexer::~Python3Lexer() { delete _interpreter; }`? As I recall, generated lexers/parsers in C++ have destructors that delete the interpreter they created in the constructor. Right — generated code pattern:\\\\n\\\\n```cpp\\\\nPython3Lexer::Python3Lexer(CharStream *input) : Lexer(input) {\\\\n  _interpreter = new atn::LexerATNSimulator(this, _atn, _decisionToDFA, _sharedContextCache);\\\\n}\\\\nPython3Lexer::~Python3Lexer() { delete _interpreter; }\\\\n```\\\\nSo the destructor deletes whatever interpreter is installed. And setInterpreter deletes the old one. Therefore no leak/double-delete. The ASan+leaks run (50 cycles including worker new/free) backs this up empirically. Good.\\\\n\\\\n2. generated_atns(): creates a throwaway lexer/parser on each worker_new call to obtain ATN pointers — a bit heavy but fine; the ATN comes from static data initialized via call_once. Note: Python3Parser's constructor creates the default interpreter (deleted? no — the throwaway parser is destructed at return and deletes its own interpreter). OK.\\\\n\\\\n3. Hold busy guard: compare_exchange_strong; released in the destructor. Exception safety: Hold is declared before try → on return/exception the destructor runs ✓. Caveat: -5 is returned when busy; but worker_free while busy is not guarded (documented: \\\\\\\"the caller must not free while a call is running\\\\\\\"). cst.jolt's close-worker uses CAS once; but close during a running parse on another thread = UB; the single-threaded guest usage assumption is disclosed in a comment. The design states a worker is used by one thread at a time. Acceptable, documented.\\\\n\\\\n4. Worker tag check: the tag field is read after static_cast — a garbage non-null pointer would be dereferenced (UB), and if that memory happens to match the tag it would be accepted. The tag reduces risk but cannot fully prevent arbitrary-pointer dereference; inherent to opaque-handle C APIs. Zeroed memory is tested (rejected). Info-level at best.\\\\n\\\\n5. worker_free: sets tag=0 then deletes — a double free would be use-after-free anyway; tag=0 does not protect against freed memory being reused. cst.jolt's idempotent close (CAS) is the real guard. Documented. OK.\\\\n\\\\n6. SourceStream BOM fix: strictDecode throws → wrapped in IllegalArgumentException (std::exception → -2 with message). Preserves U+FEFF. Used at both entry points ✓ (export :429, parse :508). Fix is correct: decode to code points itself, completely bypassing ANTLRInputStream::load. Complete? ANTLRInputStream's _data is UTF-32? In the C++ runtime ANTLRInputStream stores std::vector<uint32_t>? Actually it stores UTF-32 code points as a UTF32Array (u32 string?). strictDecode returns vector<uint32_t> — assigned to _data, p=0. size() etc. work. Reasonable. Also invalid UTF-8 → -2 \\\\\\\"illegal byte sequence\\\\\\\" — matches report (6/6 fail-closed). The oversize-limit enforcement during growth is done via room() inside Out ✓; hand_off still rechecks. room() computes kMaxOutput - buf.size() — can buf.size() exceed kMaxOutput? No: room throws before the excess; buf.size() ≤ kMaxOutput is preserved. `n > kMaxOutput - buf.size()` is unsigned and safe since buf.size() ≤ kMaxOutput. ✓.\\\\n\\\\n7. Seams gated by #ifdef; release exports are 7 (orchestrator-verified via nm). maybe_fault/fail_handler_malloc are no-ops in release ✓. Non-allocating message() is retained ✓. Oversize catch allocates nothing ✓. Busy -5 allocates nothing ✓.\\\\n\\\\n8. export_layout_version entry = 2; cst.jolt checks it at load time (to be verified in cst.jolt).\\\\n\\\\n9. Peek idiom reading private members — test seam only, reads only; no UB concern beyond the standard explicit-instantiation trick; gated.\\\\n\\\\nNext, verification: cst.jolt changes (worker handle, close-worker, layout check), stack harness child-process approach, gc log numbers, cycles log, sanitize log, workers log, input oracle/corpus, and that the round-2 oracle is unchanged + manifest is unchanged. Additionally parity 132 PASS in the log. Spot-check via grep rather than full reads to save budget.\\\\n\\\\nMain grep:\\\\n- cst.jolt: worker_new/close-worker/layout-version check.\\\\n- r4-workers-final.log: PASS count, static-growth RECORD, busy -5.\\\\n- r4-stack.log or stack-budget.edn: verify the report's numbers (main parens 10752/11264, thread 544/576, unary 93.5s@1024).\\\\n- r4-gc.log: 200ms → 271ms, 35826ms call, tick 5237.\\\\n- r4-parity-final.log: SUMMARY + 132 PASS count + input section.\\\\n- r4-sanitize.log tail: HARNESS ok exit 0.\\\\n- r4-cycles-20000.log: numbers 1,304,240 → 1,416,912.\\\\n- manifest unchanged: digest still 2483e344, etc. (should be unchanged; grep quickly).\\\\n- input oracle header is JVM-generated; corpus is 13 sources.\\\\n\\\\nLet's run these in batched greps.\\\\n\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Now spot-verify cst.jolt's worker handling and the key log numbers.\\\\n\\\\n\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_a6444ace79fe48b185d0187d\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"grep\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"worker|layout_version|close-worker|closed\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt\\\",\\\"output_mode\\\":\\\"content\\\",\\\"-n\\\":true}},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_e6054373c30c4fc69cd4fdbf\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"grep\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"10752|11264|93\\\\\\\\.5|544|576|SIGILL|SIGSEGV|unary\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r4-stack.log\\\",\\\"output_mode\\\":\\\"content\\\",\\\"-n\\\":true,\\\"head_limit\\\":40}}],\\\"meta\\\":{\\\"source\\\":\\\"model\\\",\\\"createdAt\\\":1792508170744,\\\"messageId\\\":\\\"7052a16d-7e43-4e92-b02b-07911f8e3b5d\\\"}},{\\\"role\\\":\\\"user\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_result\\\",\\\"tool_use_id\\\":\\\"call_a6444ace79fe48b185d0187d\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:4:   (`make-worker`, `parse-unit`, `parse-transform`, `utf8-bytes`,\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:17:   A worker (§7.4) holds an opaque native handle that owns the lexer and\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:20:   `close-worker` (a jolt-only addition: the other hosts' workers are\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:21:   collected). A worker serves one thread at a time; a concurrent call on the\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:22:   same worker is refused by the wrapper (status -5) and throws here.\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:24:   Disclosed limits: the native library is the one Python3 grammar the\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:27:   qualified unavailable-parser result (J5); a worker nobody closes keeps its\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:60:(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-worker-new \\\\\\\"jolt_parser_worker_new\\\\\\\" [] :pointer)\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:61:(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-worker-free \\\\\\\"jolt_parser_worker_free\\\\\\\" [:pointer] :void)\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:62:(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-export-layout-version \\\\\\\"jolt_parser_export_layout_version\\\\\\\" [] :int)\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:202:  [worker]\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:203:  (or (some-> (::native worker) deref)\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:204:      (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"jolt parser worker is closed\\\\\\\" {}))))\\\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/