{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"agent_message","text":"Reviewed `master@4d9a84a8094b3bc747a46afad7e48b3c765bbb8e` against my original findings. Shiva’s three Clojure driver files match this checkout byte for byte. I also inspected both deployed wrappers and Jupiter’s JVM runners. No suites were launched or files changed.\n\nSeveral claimed fixes are absent from this revision.\n\n**R1 — coverage and execution**\n\n- **“Run Dart’s residual” — STILL-OPEN.** The residual is now included in the emission loop at [dart_shard.clj:132](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:132), which fixes the original loop omission. However, the driver cannot load: `io/writer` at line 81 has no namespace require. `bb -e '(load-file \"src/dev/dart_shard.clj\")'` reproduced `Unable to resolve symbol: io/writer`.\n\n  Further execution blockers remain:\n  - Both namespace parsers merely drop the first output line, then consume indentation; neither seeks its lane header: [node_shard.clj:64](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:64), [dart_shard.clj:52](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:52). Actual output starts with `Read …` and `Subsystems:`, before the lane sections ([affected.clj:751](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/affected.clj:751)). A successful actual `yang.base` listing reports six CLJS namespaces; feeding that output into the current Node parser returns `[]`.\n  - Both disk scans convert absolute paths without first making them relative to the clone: [node_shard.clj:112](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:112), [dart_shard.clj:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:89). For example, `/tmp/clone/test/yang/base/foo_test.cljc` becomes `.tmp.clone.test.yang.base.foo-test`.\n  - Dart’s threaded `apply concat` at [dart_shard.clj:114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:114) splits subsystem namespace strings into characters. Its unconditional residual `slurp` also throws when no residual file exists.\n\n- **“Include JVM slow tests” — CLOSED at the wiring level.** `jupiter:~/ci/nightly-jvm.sh:67–79` now discovers namespaces and invokes `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\"` alongside the quick tier. This closes the original complete absence of the slow-tier invocation. Full-tier execution remains unvalidated.\n\n- **“Rebuild required peers at the selected SHA” — STILL-OPEN.** Node now requests both builds at [node_shard.clj:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:104). Jupiter still builds only the Node peer: `~/ci/shard-jvm.sh:12–14`; its wrapper adds no Dart-peer build. Inspection found no `build/yin-repl-peer` in Jupiter’s nightly clone. The documented JVM skip condition therefore remains ([bb.edn:250](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/bb.edn:250)).\n\n- **“Reconcile planned namespaces against fresh runtime/generated inventories, recording explicit lane exclusions” — STILL-OPEN.** The bundle’s `--list` **is authoritative for tests registered in that compiled bundle**: shadow-cljs 3.3.5’s `shadow/test/node.cljs:74–82` prints `env/get-tests`.\n\n  The pre-run check is not airtight:\n  - At [node_shard.clj:133](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:133), destructured process stdout named `out` shadows the output-directory binding. Subsequent `slurp` paths use the inventory text as their directory.\n  - The inventory command’s exit status is ignored.\n  - Only `requested − bundle` is checked; runnable bundle namespaces absent from the plan are not reconciled.\n  - Legitimate lane exclusions are not recorded.\n\n- **“Fail on unexplained missing execution” — STILL-OPEN.** Unknown requested namespaces do set the aggregate failure flag at [node_shard.clj:161](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:161), but execution has another independent defect: line 177 passes `\"--test\"` and the namespace list separately. Shadow 3.3.5 accepts only `--test=…` (`shadow/test/node.cljs:30–39`). With no recognized selector, it runs **all tests** (`:88–89`). After the preceding blockers are fixed, each supposed shard would therefore run the full bundle unless this argument is corrected.\n\n**R3 — Flutter writable state**\n\n- **“Use isolated project copies, or one Flutter invocation over all emitted files with a single concurrency budget” — CLOSED for the cache-isolation mechanism.** [dart_shard.clj:153](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:153) constructs one invocation over all collected directories, with one concurrency argument.\n\n  Shiva’s installed Flutter 3.47.1 `packages/flutter_tools/lib/src/test/test_compiler.dart:90–94` explicitly serializes compilation requests. Lines 112–129 select the shared cache; lines 277–291 update it. Multiple test workers inside this invocation do not recreate the original competing Flutter-command writers. Exclusive ownership of the clone remains necessary.\n\n- **“Treat emission failures as fatal” — CLOSED for result propagation.** At [dart_shard.clj:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:144), failed emissions set `rc=1` and their directories are excluded from the invocation. Successful directories may still run, but the aggregate cannot finish green through that branch.\n\n- **“Use fresh, run-specific output directories” — STILL-OPEN.** Both output and aggregator names have only minute precision: [dart_shard.clj:64](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:64), line 136. A same-minute rerun reuses the directory; `write-shards!` deletes the existing aggregator tree ([cljd_agg.clj:151](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/cljd_agg.clj:151)). There is no lock or exclusive directory creation.\n\n**R4 — pin protocol**\n\n- **“Atomically publish `{run-id, full-SHA, timestamp}`” — CLOSED for a single publisher.** `shiva:~/ci/nightly-full.sh:28–31` writes the requested fields and renames a temporary file in the same directory. Concurrent publishers remain unsafe because they share `nightly-pin.tmp` and have no lock.\n\n- **“Require the expected Vietnam nightly date” — CLOSED.** `jupiter:~/ci/nightly-jvm.sh:27,41–43` compares the pin date with Vietnam’s current date. Yesterday’s pin can no longer be accepted as pinned.\n\n  However, lines 35–36 stop polling upon seeing **any** `run-id=` record, including yesterday’s. Starting just before publication therefore causes immediate advisory fallback instead of waiting for today’s pin.\n\n- **“Validate the commit” — STILL-OPEN.** Lines 44–45 add the requested refetch retry, but `git cat-file -e \"$PSHA\"` checks object existence, not commit type, and the parser does not require a full-length SHA.\n\n- **“Check every Git command and actual HEAD” — STILL-OPEN.** Shiva checks fetch and checkout, but not SHA resolution/publication. Jupiter leaves fallback checkout unchecked at line 50 and never verifies actual `HEAD` against the chosen full SHA. Its initial fetch failure is logged through `fail`, then that diagnostic is overwritten by line 55.\n\n- **“Join results only by matching run-id and SHA” — STILL-OPEN.** Jupiter’s line 41 parses `20261101-0300` as just `20261101`; I reproduced this. The generation suffix is lost. A prior same-day run can be accepted, and summaries do not consistently carry the complete run ID and full SHA for reconciliation.\n\n- **“Record fallback reasons explicitly” — STILL-OPEN.** The advisory label and `RC=1` are present at lines 49–52. However, the single reason string `absent/stale/unreachable` does not identify the actual cause and also covers object-validation or checkout failures.\n\n**R6 — evidence integrity**\n\n- **“Preserve immutable per-run plans, prerequisite logs, emission logs, shard stdout/stderr, and result records” — STILL-OPEN.** The new directory layout improves retention, but minute collisions allow reuse and appending: [node_shard.clj:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:76), [dart_shard.clj:64](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:64). The summaries lack consistent full-SHA/run-ID/host/toolchain manifests.\n\n  Jupiter copies quick-tier evidence after completion (`nightly-jvm.sh:64–65`), but `shard-jvm.sh:9` still deletes its fixed evidence directory before each run. Evidence from an interrupted run can disappear before being copied.\n\n- **“Aggregate against the expected shard set” — STILL-OPEN.** Node compares completed count with a count derived from its already-filtered selection: [node_shard.clj:166](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:166), lines 187–196. An empty selection produces expected=completed=0. This does not establish completeness against an independently preserved plan. Dart has no emitted/executed set reconciliation. Jupiter’s quick tier counts existing result rows without comparing them against expected shards.\n\n- **“Return nonzero for failed, missing, or incomplete work” — STILL-OPEN across the nightly.** The Node/Dart aggregate exit paths and Shiva’s wrapper now propagate recorded failures. Jupiter still ignores the quick-runner exit status (`nightly-jvm.sh:59–63`); zero result rows yield zero red rows and can be accepted. Slow-tier count checking is an improvement, but does not repair quick-tier accounting.\n\n- **“Distinguish incomplete infrastructure from test failures” — STILL-OPEN.** Infrastructure and test failures share an undifferentiated aggregate result, and uncaught driver errors bypass completion records.\n\n- **“Sync matching manifests with bounded timeouts, keeping local evidence authoritative” — STILL-OPEN.** Both wrappers create the destination directory and retain local summaries. However, `ConnectTimeout` is applied to the preceding `ssh`, **not** the `scp` (`shiva:48–50`, `jupiter:82–84`). Neither operation has a total duration bound, and the transferred summaries lack the matching manifests required above.\n\nThe timestamp substitution does not match the description: the drivers put `stamp` in the initial line and directory name; their logging functions do not timestamp each summary line ([node_shard.clj:85](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:85), [dart_shard.clj:73](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:73)). A run timestamp plus elapsed durations could suffice; restoring shell `ts` is not itself a blocker. Unique identity, provenance, and completion evidence remain blockers.\n\n**Additional fixes checked**\n\n- **`:extra-env` preserves HOME — confirmed.** A focused probe preserved HOME. Node’s helper also merges caller additions correctly.\n- **Dart drops its slow-test setting.** [dart_shard.clj:44](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/dart_shard.clj:44) replaces caller `:extra-env` with PATH alone, discarding `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` supplied at line 156.\n- **`:err :append` remains in execution paths:** Node line 173; Dart lines 140 and 155. On both local Babashka and Shiva, a harmless `/bin/echo` succeeded with `:err :string` and failed with `:err :append`, reporting ENOENT. The prerequisite redirections were corrected; these calls were not.\n- **`mise exec -- npm ci` is correctly wired** at [node_shard.clj:100](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/node_shard.clj:100). The earlier PATH-only environment replacement is fixed.\n- **The aggregator flags are implemented:** [cljd_agg.clj:175](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/dev/cljd_agg.clj:175), lines 189 and 208–218 preserve the default compile/run path while supporting emission-only operation.\n\nThe reported 10/14 green shards remain partial evidence from an earlier implementation. They do not validate these current execution paths. The original R2 full parallel validation and clone-exclusivity concerns remain outstanding.\n\n**OVERALL: STILL-REVISE.** The Flutter cache fix, slow-tier wiring, and previous-date pin rejection close real issues. The current deployed drivers still contain reproducible startup and execution defects, and the nightly can still accept incomplete JVM work."}}
